const QS = [
{id:1,d:1,q:"You create a new application in ServiceNow Studio and reach the step for defining who can access it. Which roles has Studio already generated for you?",opts:["An admin role", "An approver role", "A user role", "A read-only role"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Admin and user. `servicenow-studio-classic/create-an-application-in-servicenow-studio.md`: \"ServiceNow Studio automatically defines default admin and user roles. You can remove the predefined roles or add more roles.\" Approver and read-only roles exist only if you author them. Corroborated by `app-engine-studio/app-tutorial-create-an-app.md`."},
{id:3,d:1,q:"A platform developer wants to create app files, edit metadata, and package the application for deployment without leaving a single environment. Which tool is built for that?",opts:["Application Repository", "ServiceNow Studio", "Platform Analytics", "Instance Scan"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"ServiceNow Studio. `servicenow-studio-classic/exploring-servicenow-studio.md` states the point twice: \"manage app files, edit metadata, and package changes for deployment without switching between tools.\" Application Repository *distributes* finished apps rather than building them; Platform Analytics and Instance Scan are unrelated."},
{id:4,d:1,q:"You have an application file open in ServiceNow Studio. Which two pieces of context does the status bar display?",opts:["The application scope", "The update set the changes are being captured into", "The Git branch the application is linked to", "The delegated developer assigned to the file"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Scope and update set. `servicenow-studio-classic/servicenow-studio-user-interface.md`: \"When you have an app or file open, the status bar shows the current scope and the update set associated with the application.\" These are the two settings that silently misfile your work when wrong."},
{id:5,d:1,q:"Which two are capabilities of ServiceNow Studio?",opts:["Opening and editing files in any scope you have access to, including global", "Developing an application while disconnected from the instance", "Linking an application to a source control repository", "Encrypting scripts so that other developers on the instance cannot read them"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Any-scope editing and source control. `exploring-servicenow-studio.md` lists \"Global and custom scope: Open and edit files of any scope you have access to\"; source control has its own page (`link-app-to-source-control.md`). Trap on D: a Script Protection Policy genuinely exists (`c_ScriptProtectionPolicy.md`), but it protects scripts in *published store apps* — it is not a Studio feature and does not hide code from developers on your own instance. Studio is browser-based, so B is impossible."},
{id:8,d:1,q:"ServiceNow Studio gives you one place to do which three things with application files?",opts:["Create them", "Review them", "Update them", "Automatically generate test suites for them", "Translate them into other languages", "Attach them to a change request"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Create, review, update. `exploring-servicenow-studio.md`: \"Create, edit, maintain, and deploy custom, globally scoped, and base system apps and app files.\" Test generation, translation and change-request attachment are other tools' jobs."},
{id:10,d:1,q:"Which table stores the definitions of custom many-to-many relationships?",opts:["sys_m2m", "sys_collection", "sys_relationship", "sys_m2m_definition"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`sys_m2m`. `platform-administration/table-administration-and-data-management/t_CreateAManyToManyRelationship.md`: \"The Many to Many Definitions `[sys_m2m]` table allows administrators to create custom many-to-many relationships.\""},
{id:11,d:1,q:"An administrator wants to browse the many-to-many relationships that ship with the base system. What should they enter in the navigation filter?",opts:["sys_m2m.list", "sys_collection.list", "sys_relationship.list", "sys_m2m_base.list"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`sys_collection.list`. `r_RefDefaultManyToManyRels.md`: \"To reference many-to-many relationships that are available in the base system, administrators can enter `sys_collection.list` in the navigation filter.\" Trap: Q10 and Q11 are a deliberate pair — the same doc adds \"Only use this table to view many-to-many relationships in the base system. To create a new relationship, always use the Many-to-Many Definitions table.\" So `sys_m2m` = create, `sys_collection` = view what already ships."},
{id:12,d:2,q:"What does a formatter put on a form?",opts:["Information that is not a field on the record", "A read-only copy of a field from a referenced record", "A calculated value derived from two or more fields", "A validation message raised when the form fails to save"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Information that is not a field on the record. `platform-administration/c_Formatters.md`: \"A formatter is a form element used to display information that is not a field in the record.\" Activity stream, Approvers and Process Flow are all formatters. Worth knowing: a *custom* formatter is a UI macro plus a formatter record that points at it."},
{id:13,d:2,q:"What does an embedded list let a user do?",opts:["View and edit related records without navigating away from the form", "Export the form's data to a CSV file", "Display a field from a referenced record inline", "Attach a document to the record"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"View and edit related records inline. `platform-user-interface/configure-use-list-functions.md`: \"Some lists may be embedded in forms. Use list controls to work with records in an embedded list within a form\" — you can insert, expand and collapse rows without leaving the form. Trap on C: displaying a single field from a referenced record is a *derived/dot-walked field*, not an embedded list."},
{id:15,d:2,q:"In the Next Experience navigation, which element do you open to reach any item in the instance?",opts:["The contextual application pill", "The All menu", "The Favorites menu", "The History menu"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"The All menu. `platform-user-interface/adoption-services/configure-next-experience-onboarding.md` enumerates the Next Experience elements as Intro/welcome, Pinning, All menu, Favorites menu, History and Application Pill — so All menu and the pill are distinct things. All menu is the full catalogue of everything in the instance."},
{id:16,d:2,q:"In the Next Experience navigation, which element shows you where you currently are in the instance and lets you favorite the item you are viewing?",opts:["The History menu", "The All menu", "The contextual application pill", "The Favorites menu"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"The contextual application pill. Same source as Q15. Q15/Q16 are a matched pair and cannot share an answer: *reach anything* = All menu; *where am I, and favorite it* = application pill. Favorites and History are for items you already saved or visited."},
{id:22,d:3,q:"Deny-Unless access controls are evaluated before Allow-If access controls. What does that ordering achieve?",opts:["The instance restricts by default, so a failing Deny-Unless blocks the request before anything can grant it", "Access control evaluation completes in fewer database queries", "Roles assigned directly to a user outrank roles inherited from a group", "Idle user sessions are terminated sooner"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Secure by default. `platform-security/access-control/acl-denial-behavior.md`: \"Deny-Unless ACLs will take priority against Allow-If ACLs in ACL Evaluation, as it will be evaluated first.\" Putting the restrictive type first means a failing Deny-Unless vetoes the request before any Allow-If gets a chance to grant it. B, C and D invent performance and session rationales."},
{id:23,d:3,q:"The access control engine encounters a rule that is empty or invalid. What does it do by default?",opts:["Grants access for the remainder of the session", "Falls back to a system-supplied default rule", "Denies access", "Sends an approval request to an administrator"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Deny. `exploring-access-control-list.md`: \"By default the ACL engine completely denies access if an ACL is empty or invalid.\" ServiceNow fails closed — the single most reliable access-control principle on this exam. An ACL counts as *empty* when it lacks a role, security attribute, data condition and script."},
{id:24,d:3,q:"Which statement describes both access control decision types correctly?",opts:["Deny-Unless denies access unless its requirements are met; Allow-If grants access if its requirements are met", "Deny-Unless denies access unconditionally; Allow-If grants access unconditionally", "Deny-Unless grants access by default; Allow-If ignores its conditions entirely", "Both grant access as soon as any one requirement is present"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Read it literally. `exploring-access-control-list.md` defines Deny-Unless as \"restrict access to resource by explicitly denying access unless conditions are passed\" and Allow-If as \"allow access to resource if conditions are passed.\" A restates both; B, C and D each break one half."},
{id:25,d:3,q:"An administrator builds an access control but specifies neither a role nor a security attribute. What does the system do?",opts:["Prompts them to select a role or an existing security attribute", "Saves the record and writes a warning to the system log", "Refuses to save the record at all", "Assigns the admin role automatically"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"It prompts you. `exploring-access-control-list.md`: \"If the system detects the user creating an ACL it will prompt the user to select a role or an existing security attribute.\" It neither blocks the save (C) nor silently accepts it (B) nor guesses a role (D)."},
{id:26,d:3,q:"Which access control decision type is evaluated first?",opts:["Allow-If", "Deny-Unless", "Rules that specify a script", "Rules that specify no role"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Deny-Unless. Same source as Q22, asked as sequence rather than rationale. C and D are not decision types — they describe what an individual rule contains."},
{id:27,d:3,q:"Which of these makes an access control *invalid*, rather than merely restrictive?",opts:["It requires a role that exists but is currently inactive", "It references a security attribute that has no record in the database", "It requires a role that no user currently holds", "Its condition evaluates to false for every record on the table"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A security attribute with no database record. `exploring-access-control-list.md` defines invalid ACLs as those with roles that do not exist, security attributes that do not exist, or a script containing `answer=true` or `true`. The discrimination: invalid means *unresolvable*, not *ineffective*. An inactive role, an unheld role and an always-false condition are all valid references that simply grant nobody access."},
{id:28,d:3,q:"Which two together stop users from reporting on list report columns that hold sensitive data?",opts:["The report_view table access control", "The add_to_list access control", "Setting the glide.report.add_to_list_supported property to true", "The read operation access control", "Setting the glide.report.report_view.check_published property to true"],ans:[1, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"The add_to_list ACL plus its enabling property. `now-intelligence/reporting/column-view-access-control-list-reports.md`: \"the glide.report.add_to_list_supported system property enables the add_to_list access control list. This access control list (ACL) prevents users from reporting on list report columns with sensitive data.\" The property is inactive by default, so the ACL alone does nothing. Trap on E: `glide.report.report_view.check_published` is a real property (`sc-enable-report-view-acls.md`) but it governs *viewing published reports*, not *building* them."},
{id:29,d:3,q:"A manager wants to stop users from picking the caller_name field as a column when they build a new report. Which operation's access control does that?",opts:["report_view", "add_to_list", "list_edit", "read"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"add_to_list. Same doc: \"If a table field is restricted for the user, it doesn't appear in the Available column. Users are therefore not able to select it in the reports they create.\" Learn the split — `add_to_list` = can you pick the field while building; `report_view` = can you see it once built. Two limits worth knowing: the ACL applies only to the top-level table the report is based on, and existing reports are unaffected."},
{id:32,d:3,q:"Which two are documented benefits of Access Analyzer?",opts:["It provides reporting capabilities for the analyzer's results", "It helps you achieve least-privilege when implementing access controls", "It encrypts sensitive field data at rest", "It records which pages anonymous users were able to reach", "It replaces access controls with a role-only permission model"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Both are listed verbatim under Benefits in `platform-security/access-control/explore-access-analyzer.md`: \"Provide reporting capabilities for the analyzer results\" and \"Achieve the least privilege principals when implementing access controls.\" Note the tool's current scope — it validates access for resources and agentic assets (agentic workflows and AI agents), which is a rebuild from earlier releases."},
{id:34,d:6,q:"On an instance where the App Collaboration Component is *not* installed, where do you go to grant someone delegated developer permissions on an application?",opts:["The application record, then Manage Developers", "The application record, then Roles Required", "The role record, then Subscribed Users", "The System Applications menu, then Delegated Developers"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Application record → Manage Developers. `delegated-development-and-deployment/t_AddADeveloper.md` walks it: All > System Applications > My Company Applications, open the application, \"Select Manage Developers. Note: If the App Collaboration Component is installed, the link appears as Manage Collaborators.\" Two facts worth carrying: developer permissions exist only for scoped apps, not global, and if Application administration is enabled only an application administrator can delegate. Trap on B: Roles Required governs who can *use* the app, not who can *develop* it."},
{id:35,d:6,q:"Which action on a role record makes that role usable as an application-specific administrator?",opts:["Add the role to the Application Administrator related list", "Select the Application Administrator check box and update the role record", "Add the role to an Application Administrator group", "Select the Delegated Application Developer check box and update the role record"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Select the Application Administrator check box. `application-administration.md`: \"You can make any role an application-specific administrator by selecting the Application Administrator check box in Role Configuration.\" `enable-application-administration.md` adds that this check box replaced the older Assignable by field. D is the same sentence with a fabricated field name — read these options word by word."},
{id:36,d:6,q:"Which property name sets the minimum number of application administrators an application must retain?",opts:["admin_count", "num_admin", "max_admin_count", "min_admin_count"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`min_admin_count`, namespaced to the application's scope — e.g. `sn_uni_task.min_admin_count`, documented as ensuring \"a minimum number of scoped administrators are active at any given time for managing the application\" (`employee-service-management/universal-task/assign-ut-admin-role.md`). `max_admin_count` is the mirror-image trap; there is no cap, only a floor."},
{id:37,d:6,q:"An application requires a minimum of four application administrators. It currently has two. What can be done?",opts:["Two or more application administrators can be added", "Exactly one application administrator can be added", "One application administrator can be removed", "All but one application administrator can be removed"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Add two or more. The count is *below* the configured minimum, so the only permitted direction is up, and it takes at least two to reach four. Every removal option is blocked — the whole point of the property is that the application cannot be locked out by losing its administrators. Trap on B: \"exactly one\" misreads a floor as a quota."},
{id:38,d:6,q:"In App Engine Studio, which role can assign sharing permissions for the templates that user creates?",opts:["sn_app_eng_studio.user", "app_template_author", "app_template_admin", "scan_admin"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"app_template_author — authors control sharing for the templates *they* create. `app-engine-studio/manage-template-access.md`."},
{id:39,d:6,q:"In App Engine Studio, which role can review templates, activate and deactivate them, and assign sharing permissions across *all* templates?",opts:["app_template_author", "delegated_developer", "app_template_admin", "scan_admin"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"app_template_admin — admins review, activate/deactivate, and control sharing across all templates. Same doc. Q38/Q39 are a scope pair: author = own templates, admin = every template."},
{id:40,d:1,q:"A developer creates a new scoped application with the namespace `x_acme_fleet`. They then create a table named `vehicle` inside that application. What will the actual table name be?",opts:["`u_vehicle`", "`vehicle`", "`x_acme_fleet_vehicle`", "`sys_x_acme_fleet_vehicle`"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Scoped tables are prefixed with the application namespace: `x_acme_fleet_vehicle`. `u_` is the global-scope custom prefix, so A is the classic wrong answer. `sys_` is reserved for platform tables."},
{id:41,d:1,q:"Which statement about the `u_` and `x_` table-name prefixes is correct?",opts:["`u_` marks a table created in a scoped application; `x_` marks one created in the global scope", "`u_` marks a custom table created in the global scope; `x_` marks a table created in a scoped application", "Both prefixes mean the same thing and are assigned at random", "`u_` is applied to all custom tables regardless of scope, and `x_` is applied only to tables from the ServiceNow Store"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`u_` = custom table in global scope. `x_` = table in a scoped app. Seeing `u_` on a table tells you immediately it is global."},
{id:42,d:1,q:"A requirement states that a new Vehicle Request record must be assignable, have a state, carry an SLA, and appear in existing task reporting. What is the best data-model decision?",opts:["Create a brand-new table with no parent and manually add assignment and state fields", "Extend the Task [task] table", "Extend the Configuration Item [cmdb_ci] table", "Add the fields directly to the Task table itself"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Assignment, state, SLA and task reporting are all Task behaviours — extend Task and inherit them. D is the trap: adding fields to Task itself pollutes every task-derived table platform-wide."},
{id:43,d:1,q:"Which is the strongest indicator that a business requirement is a good fit for building on ServiceNow?",opts:["The process is entirely computational with no human participants and needs sub-millisecond latency", "The work is task-based, moves through states, needs approvals, and involves people across teams", "The requirement is to store and stream large binary media files to external consumers", "The requirement is to render a high-frame-rate 3D graphics interface"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"ServiceNow fits task-based, stateful, approval-driven, cross-team work. A, C and D are the canonical poor fits (real-time compute, media streaming, heavy graphics)."},
{id:44,d:1,q:"In a many-to-many relationship between two tables, what does ServiceNow actually create to store the relationship?",opts:["A reference field on each of the two tables", "A new table that holds a reference to each of the two related tables", "A related list definition only, with no underlying table", "A Document ID field on the parent table"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"M2M creates an intermediate table holding one reference to each side. A describes one-to-many done twice; C and D describe unrelated constructs."},
{id:45,d:1,q:"Which two are true of a module in an application menu?",opts:["A module can open a list, a form, a URL, or a separate script", "A module must always point to a table", "A module's visibility can be restricted with roles", "A module can only be created by the System Administrator and cannot be scoped"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Modules can open lists, forms, URLs, or run a script, and can be role-restricted. B is false (a module need not target a table) and D is false (modules are scoped like any app file)."},
{id:46,d:1,q:"What does setting Runtime access tracking to Enforcing on an application do?",opts:["Logs cross-scope access attempts but permits all of them", "Permits only cross-scope runtime requests that have a valid cross-scope privilege record", "Disables all cross-scope access unconditionally", "Automatically creates and approves any cross-scope privilege record on demand"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Enforcing = only requests with a valid cross-scope privilege record run. Tracking is the log-but-allow mode described in A — that is the distinction being tested."},
{id:47,d:1,q:"Which table stores cross-scope privilege records?",opts:["`sys_scope`", "`sys_scope_privilege`", "`sys_app_privilege`", "`sys_cross_scope`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`sys_scope_privilege`. The other three are invented table names."},
{id:48,d:1,q:"In a cross-scope privilege record, which operations are supported for a script include target?",opts:["read, write, create, delete", "execute API only", "read and execute only", "All CRUD operations plus execute API"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Script includes and script objects support execute API only. Tables are the ones supporting read/write/create/delete — do not swap them."},
{id:49,d:1,q:"A developer wants other developers to see the logic inside their published script include but not modify it. Which script protection policy should they choose?",opts:["None", "Read-only", "Protected", "Restricted"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Read-only = others can see the logic but not change it."},
{id:50,d:1,q:"Which script protection policy prevents other application developers from changing your intellectual property?",opts:["None", "Read-only", "Protected", "Private"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Protected = IP protection, logic not changeable. Q49/Q50 are a pair: Read-only exposes the code, Protected does not. \"Restricted\"/\"Private\" are not policy values."},
{id:51,d:1,q:"An application is published with Can Edit Application in Studio set to false. What is the consequence for someone who downloads it?",opts:["They cannot install the application at all", "They cannot edit the application in Studio, but retain access to Source Control integration features inside Studio", "They can edit the application freely; the setting only affects the publishing instance", "They can edit the application only after requesting access from the publisher"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"They lose Studio editing but keep Source Control integration features inside Studio. The setting has no effect on apps still in development on the publishing instance."},
{id:52,d:1,q:"Which statement about the global scope is correct?",opts:["Global applications cannot access tables in scoped applications under any circumstance", "Global applications can alter data you do not intend to alter, so scoped apps are preferred for new tables", "Global scope automatically applies a namespace prefix to all new tables", "Global scope is required in order to use Delegated Development"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Straight from the docs: global apps can alter data you do not intend to alter, so use scoped apps for new tables. Global scope does not add a namespace prefix (C), and Delegated Development does not require global (D)."},
{id:53,d:1,q:"By default, what access does a scoped application have to its own tables and business logic?",opts:["None until explicitly granted", "Read-only", "Full access to read and change them", "Access only through cross-scope privilege records"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A scoped app has full access to its own tables and logic by default; it is other applications that need explicit permission."},
{id:54,d:1,q:"Which two places let an administrator specify what parts of an application are accessible to other applications?",opts:["The Custom application record", "Each application Table record", "The Update Set record", "The Application Menu record"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"The Custom application record and each application Table record. Update sets and application menus have nothing to do with cross-application accessibility."},
{id:55,d:1,q:"A developer creates a table that extends Task. Which statement about the resulting fields is correct?",opts:["The child table gets a private copy of the Task fields that can be renamed freely", "The child table inherits the Task fields, and records appear in Task-level lists and reports", "The child table inherits no fields; extension only affects ACLs", "The child table inherits fields but its records do not appear in Task queries"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Extension inherits fields and makes records visible at the parent level — that is exactly why Q42's answer is \"extend Task\"."},
{id:56,d:1,q:"What is the effect of the Application Access setting \"Can read\" on a scoped table when accessed from another scope?",opts:["It grants read access to all users regardless of ACLs", "It permits other applications to read the table, still subject to ACLs", "It replaces the need for ACLs entirely", "It only affects REST API access"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Application Access controls cross-scope reachability; ACLs still apply on top. A and C both wrongly treat it as an ACL bypass."},
{id:57,d:1,q:"Which tool is designed to walk a developer through creating an application by defining the app record, roles, and data tables in a guided sequence?",opts:["ServiceNow Studio", "Guided Application Creator", "Table Builder", "Update Set Manager"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Guided Application Creator — app record, roles, data tables (see Q7). Studio is the IDE, not a guided sequence."},
{id:58,d:1,q:"Which statement best distinguishes App Engine Studio from ServiceNow Studio?",opts:["They are the same product under two names", "App Engine Studio is a low-code guided experience; ServiceNow Studio is the full IDE for application files", "App Engine Studio is the full IDE; ServiceNow Studio is the low-code guided experience", "App Engine Studio can only edit existing apps and never create new ones"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"AES = low-code guided app building; Studio = full IDE over application files. D is a distortion of a real fact: AES User Limited group members can only edit existing apps."},
{id:59,d:1,q:"A developer needs a field that stores one of a fixed list of values chosen by the user. Which field type is appropriate?",opts:["String with a Choice list defined", "Reference", "Document ID", "Glide List"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A String field with a Choice list. Reference points at records in another table, which is a different requirement."},
{id:60,d:1,q:"What distinguishes a Reference field from a Glide List field?",opts:["Reference points to exactly one record; Glide List can point to multiple records on the same table", "Reference points to multiple records; Glide List points to one", "Reference works only on Task tables; Glide List works anywhere", "There is no functional difference"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Reference = one record. Glide List = many records on one table."},
{id:61,d:1,q:"Which statement about the Document ID field type is correct?",opts:["It references a single fixed table chosen at design time", "It references a record on a table determined at runtime by a companion table-name field", "It stores a URL to an external document", "It stores an attachment sys_id"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Document ID resolves its target table at runtime via a companion table field — that is what makes it different from Reference."},
{id:62,d:1,q:"An application must be installable on other instances and versioned over time. Which is the appropriate distribution mechanism?",opts:["Update Set exported to XML", "Publish the application to the Application Repository", "Copy and paste the scripts manually", "A scheduled data export"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"The Application Repository handles versioned install/upgrade across instances. Update sets move changes between dev/test/prod, not distribute a versioned product."},
{id:63,d:1,q:"Which two statements about application scope and naming conflicts are correct?",opts:["The namespace identifier prevents naming conflicts between applications", "Scope allows the contextual development environment to determine which changes are permitted", "Scope guarantees that two applications can never reference the same table", "Scope removes the need for unique field names within a single table"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Namespace prevents conflicts; scope drives what the contextual development environment permits. C overstates it (cross-scope references are possible when granted) and D is unrelated."},
{id:64,d:1,q:"A developer is working in Studio and notices the status bar shows a different application than the one they intend to edit. What is the risk if they proceed?",opts:["No risk; Studio automatically reassigns files on save", "New application files will be created in the wrong scope", "The instance will reject the save with an error", "The files will be created correctly but placed in the wrong update set only"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Files land in whatever scope is active — Studio will not stop you. This is why Q4/Q6 (scope in the status bar and browser tab) are worth knowing."},
{id:65,d:2,q:"Which script type runs in the user's browser and can respond to a field value changing without a round trip to the server?",opts:["Business Rule", "Client Script", "Script Include", "Scheduled Script Execution"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Client Scripts are the browser-side tier. Business rules and script includes are server-side; scheduled executions have no form context at all."},
{id:66,d:2,q:"Which client script type runs when a form is loaded and the fields are populated?",opts:["onChange", "onSubmit", "onLoad", "onCellEdit"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"onLoad runs once as the form renders, after fields are populated. Contrast the four types: onLoad (form opens), onChange (a field value changes), onSubmit (form is saved), onCellEdit (value changed in a list)."},
{id:67,d:2,q:"Which client script type runs when a value is changed in a list view rather than on a form?",opts:["onChange", "onCellEdit", "onSubmit", "onLoad"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"onCellEdit is the list-view type. Candidates routinely answer onChange here — onChange is the form equivalent."},
{id:68,d:2,q:"A requirement is to make a field mandatory and read-only based on the value of another field, with no scripting. What should be used?",opts:["Client Script", "UI Policy", "Business Rule", "Data Policy"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"UI Policy is the declarative way to set mandatory/read-only/visible. Reaching for a Client Script here is the classic over-engineering trap."},
{id:69,d:2,q:"What is the key difference between a UI Policy and a Data Policy?",opts:["UI Policy enforces rules on the form only; Data Policy enforces rules on data regardless of entry point, including imports and web services", "Data Policy applies to the form only; UI Policy applies everywhere", "They are identical but Data Policy is deprecated", "UI Policy runs on the server; Data Policy runs on the client"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"UI Policy = form only. Data Policy = enforced regardless of entry point, including import sets and web services. The most testable UI-vs-Data distinction there is."},
{id:70,d:2,q:"Which two statements about UI Policies versus Client Scripts are correct?",opts:["UI Policies run after Client Scripts on form load", "UI Policies are the preferred no-code way to set mandatory, read-only, and visible", "Client Scripts should be preferred over UI Policies for simple field-state changes", "Client Scripts run only on the server"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"UI Policies run after client scripts on load and are the preferred declarative option. C inverts best practice; D is false."},
{id:71,d:2,q:"Which method correctly retrieves the value of a field in a client script?",opts:["`current.getValue('short_description')`", "`g_form.getValue('short_description')`", "`gs.getValue('short_description')`", "`g_scratchpad.short_description`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`g_form.getValue()`. `current` and `gs` are server-side; `g_scratchpad` is a data carrier, not an accessor."},
{id:72,d:2,q:"A developer needs data computed on the server made available to a client script when a form loads, without an extra server call. What should they use?",opts:["A display business rule writing to `g_scratchpad`", "An after business rule writing to `g_scratchpad`", "GlideAjax from an onLoad client script", "A before business rule writing to `current`"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A display business rule runs before the form is presented and can populate `g_scratchpad`. An after rule (B) is too late. GlideAjax (C) works but costs the extra server call the question excludes."},
{id:73,d:2,q:"Which two statements about `g_scratchpad` are correct?",opts:["It is populated on the server and read on the client", "It is populated on the client and read on the server", "It is typically set in a display business rule", "It is typically set in an after business rule"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Server writes it, client reads it, set in a display business rule. B reverses the direction; D names the wrong rule type."},
{id:74,d:2,q:"A client script must call server-side logic and use the result. What is the correct mechanism?",opts:["Call the script include directly by name from the client script", "Use GlideAjax against a script include marked Client callable", "Use `gs.include()` in the client script", "Use a before business rule"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"GlideAjax + a Client callable script include. Client scripts cannot invoke server code directly (A), and `gs` does not exist on the client (C)."},
{id:75,d:2,q:"For a script include to be callable via GlideAjax, what must be true?",opts:["The Client callable checkbox must be selected", "It must extend `AbstractAjaxProcessor`", "It must be in the global scope", "It must be marked as a Scheduled Job"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Client callable checked and extends `AbstractAjaxProcessor`. Scope is irrelevant (C); Scheduled Job is a different artifact (D)."},
{id:76,d:2,q:"Which statement about a Record Producer is correct?",opts:["It is a report format for catalog data", "It is a Service Catalog item that presents a friendly form and creates a record on a target table", "It can only create records on the Incident table", "It replaces the need for ACLs on the target table"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A catalog item that produces a record on a target table — that is how it serves as an application UI. Works with any table (C false); does not affect ACLs (D false)."},
{id:77,d:2,q:"In a Record Producer script, which variable represents the record being created?",opts:["`g_form`", "`producer`", "`current`", "`g_scratchpad`"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`current` is the record being created."},
{id:78,d:2,q:"In a Record Producer script, how do you access the value a user entered into a producer variable?",opts:["`current.variable_name`", "`producer.variablename`", "`g_form.getValue('variable_name')`", "`gs.getProperty('variable_name')`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Producer variables are read from the `producer` object. Q77/Q78 pair up: `current` = the record, `producer` = the user's input. Mixing them is the intended error."},
{id:79,d:2,q:"Which client-side scripts apply to Record Producers?",opts:["Catalog Client Scripts and Catalog UI Policies", "UI Scripts and UI Actions", "UI Scripts and Record Producer Scripts", "Client Scripts and UI Policies"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Record Producers are catalog artifacts, so they use Catalog Client Scripts and Catalog UI Policies — not the ordinary form-based ones."},
{id:80,d:2,q:"What is the fastest way to create and configure a Record Producer for an existing table?",opts:["Create a Catalog Category and select Add New Record Producer", "Use the Record Producer module and add every variable manually", "Open the table in Tables and select the Add to Service Catalog related link", "Right-click the form header and select Create Record Producer"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Add to Service Catalog, verbatim from `servicenow-platform/service-catalog/t_CreatingRecordProducersFromTables.md`: \"Navigate to All > System Definition > Tables and open the table record… Under Related Links, select Add to Service Catalog.\" You then pick the fields and their order, and the producer plus its variables are generated for you."},
{id:81,d:2,q:"Which two are legitimate uses of a UI Action?",opts:["Add a button to a form that runs server-side script", "Add a context-menu item to a list", "Enforce a field as mandatory on import", "Schedule a script to run nightly"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Form buttons and list context-menu items are both UI Actions. C is a Data Policy job; D is a Scheduled Script Execution."},
{id:82,d:2,q:"Which of the following is NOT a UI Action type?",opts:["List choice", "Form button", "List banner button", "Form choice"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Form choice is not a UI Action type. List choice, Form button and List banner button all are. ⚠️ Heavily contested: mined sources keyed this as \"List choice\" and also listed \"Form choice\" as *valid* in a different item. The `sys_ui_action` table has List choice; it has no Form choice."},
{id:83,d:2,q:"Which statement about form Views is correct?",opts:["A view is a named arrangement of fields and sections that can differ by role or device", "A view changes the underlying table schema", "Only one view can exist per table", "Views are stored as separate physical tables"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Views are named field/section arrangements and can vary by role or device. They are presentation only — the schema is untouched."},
{id:84,d:2,q:"What is the purpose of a Related List on a form?",opts:["To display records from another table that reference this record", "To display a read-only copy of the current record's fields", "To create a many-to-many relationship automatically", "To restrict which fields a user can edit"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A related list shows records from another table that reference this one."},
{id:85,d:2,q:"Which two are true of the `g_form` API?",opts:["`g_form.setMandatory()` makes a field required on the form", "`g_form.showFieldMsg()` displays a message beside a specific field", "`g_form.query()` runs a database query directly from the client", "`g_form.setAbortAction()` deletes the current record"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"`setMandatory` and `showFieldMsg` are real. `g_form.query()` does not exist, and `setAbortAction` belongs to server-side `current`, never deletes anything."},
{id:86,d:2,q:"Which client script would you use to stop a form from being submitted when a validation fails?",opts:["onLoad", "onChange", "onSubmit returning false", "onCellEdit"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"onSubmit returning false halts submission."},
{id:87,d:2,q:"What is the correct way to abort a record operation from a before business rule?",opts:["`return false;`", "`current.setAbortAction(true);`", "`g_form.setAbortAction(true);`", "`gs.abort();`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`current.setAbortAction(true)` on the server. `return false` is the *client* onSubmit idiom — mixing the tiers is exactly what Q86/Q87 test."},
{id:88,d:2,q:"Which two are recommended debugging strategies for client-side scripts?",opts:["`g_form.addInfoMessage()`", "`jslog()`", "`gs.log()`", "`gs.addErrorMessage()`"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"`g_form.addInfoMessage()` and `jslog()` are client-side. `gs.*` is server-side and unavailable in a client script — that is why gs.log() is the stock wrong answer in every version of this item."},
{id:89,d:2,q:"A field must be hidden from users without a specific role, and the restriction must hold even if the record is accessed through the REST API. What is the correct mechanism?",opts:["UI Policy", "Client Script", "ACL on the field", "Form layout"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Only an ACL enforces server-side and therefore survives REST access. UI Policy, Client Script and form layout are cosmetic — the highest-value security instinct on the exam."},
{id:90,d:2,q:"In Form Designer, if you edit the label of a field on a child table, which table is updated?",opts:["The base table", "The parent table", "The child table only", "All tables in the hierarchy"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The child table only. Labels are per-table overrides, so the parent and its other children are unaffected."},
{id:91,d:2,q:"Which statement about the Form Designer is correct?",opts:["To add a field to the form layout, drag it from the Fields tab to the form", "To add a section to the form layout, drag it from the Field Types tab", "Removing a field from the form layout deletes it from the database table", "New fields cannot be created from the Form Designer"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The field navigator has exactly two tabs (`platform-administration/c_FormDesign.md`): Fields — \"a list of existing fields you can add to the current form\" — and Field Types — \"a list of field types… Adding a field type to the form layout creates fields in the selected table when the form layout is saved.\" So A is right, and D is wrong because new fields *can* be created here. C is wrong: removing a field from the layout never deletes it from the table. ⚠️ Correction: an earlier version of this note claimed sections also come from the Field Types tab. They do not — a section is added with the (+) button beside the section label, then dragged only to reorder. That is why B fails."},
{id:92,d:2,q:"What happens to the List view when a new field is added to an existing table?",opts:["The new field is added at the end of the list", "A new list view is created containing the new field", "The new field is added at the start of the list", "The new field is not added to the list view"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Adding a field to a table does not add it to the list view — list layout is configured separately."},
{id:93,d:2,q:"Can inherited fields be deleted from a child table?",opts:["Yes, using the red X in the table definition", "Yes, but only text fields", "No, inherited fields cannot be deleted from a child table", "Yes, but only if no data has ever been saved"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Inherited fields cannot be deleted from a child table; they belong to the parent. Compare Q42/Q55 — inheritance is a benefit and a constraint."},
{id:94,d:2,q:"Which field type would you select to query records from another table on a form?",opts:["Date", "Phone Number", "String", "Reference"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Reference field."},
{id:95,d:2,q:"Which is a good practice for adding instructions to a form?",opts:["Annotations", "Related links to wiki pages", "A context menu UI Action", "A populated read-only field"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Annotations are the supported way to put instructional text on a form."},
{id:96,d:2,q:"If the Create module option is selected when creating a table, what is the new module's default behavior?",opts:["Open an empty form so new records can be created", "Open a link to a wiki article", "Display an empty homepage for the application", "Display a list of all records from the table"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The generated module displays a list of all records from the table."},
{id:97,d:2,q:"Which of these is not available to a client-side script?",opts:["GlideAjax", "GlideForm", "GlideUser", "GlideSystem"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"GlideSystem (`gs`) is server-only — it never appears in the client API listing. GlideAjax, GlideForm and GlideUser are all listed as Client APIs in `api-reference/api-client.md`. ⚠️ This question was rewritten after an audit found the previous version wrong. It asked which class is \"NOT part of the client-side scoped APIs\" and keyed GlideRecord — but `api-client.md` explicitly lists \"GlideRecord - Client… enables the use of some GlideRecord functionality in client-side scripts, such as client scripts and UI policy scripts.\" Client-side GlideRecord exists; it is *discouraged* for performance, which is not the same as unavailable. See Q294."},
{id:98,d:3,q:"Within a single ACL, in what order are the elements evaluated?",opts:["Conditions, Roles, Script", "Script, Conditions, Roles", "Role, Security Attribute, Condition, Script", "Conditions, Script, Roles"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Current documentation is explicit: within an ACL the hierarchy is Role → Security Attribute → Condition → Script. ⚠️ Read the options carefully on exam day. Older course material taught \"Conditions, Roles, Script\" and dump sites still key it that way — the two most-copied dumps contradict each other on this exact item. Prefer a role-first option when one is offered."},
{id:99,d:3,q:"Which three conditions make an ACL invalid?",opts:["An ACL with a role that does not exist in the database", "An ACL with a Security Attribute that does not exist", "An ACL whose script contains only `answer=true` or `true`", "An ACL with a role that exists but is inactive", "An ACL that references a deprecated API"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"docs",exp:"Documented invalid-ACL definition: non-existent role, non-existent security attribute, or a script containing just `answer=true`/`true`. D is the trap — an inactive role still exists, so the ACL is valid but ineffective. Compare Q27."},
{id:100,d:3,q:"When ServiceNow evaluates Access Controls for a requested object, in what order does it search for matching rules?",opts:["Only for matches on the current table", "Only for matches on the current field", "From the most specific match to the most generic match", "From the most generic match to the most specific match"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Most specific → most generic. This is why a field ACL is found before the table wildcard."},
{id:101,d:3,q:"For a record operation, which statement about table-level and field-level ACL rules is correct?",opts:["Only the field-level rule needs to evaluate to true", "Only the table-level rule needs to evaluate to true", "The matching table-level and field-level rules must both evaluate to true", "Whichever rule is evaluated first determines the outcome"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Both must evaluate to true. A field is only readable if the table-level rule and the field-level rule pass."},
{id:102,d:3,q:"An ACL rule has an empty Required roles list. How does the role portion of that ACL evaluate?",opts:["It always fails, denying access", "It evaluates to true", "It prompts the administrator to add a role", "It grants access only to admin"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"An empty roles list evaluates to true — it imposes no role requirement. Candidates routinely assume empty means deny."},
{id:103,d:3,q:"If the system finds no matching access control rules at all for a requested object and operation, what happens by default?",opts:["Access is denied", "Access is granted", "The request is queued for administrator approval", "The system throws an error"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Documented behaviour: no matching rules → access is granted. The platform ships default rules so this is rare in practice, which is why the intuition \"no ACL means denied\" feels right and is wrong. Contrast Q23: an *empty or invalid* ACL denies; *no matching* ACL grants."},
{id:104,d:3,q:"Which system property controls whether the wildcard table ACL rules deny or allow read, write, create, and delete across all tables?",opts:["`glide.security.default_deny`", "`glide.sm.default_mode`", "`glide.acl.wildcard_mode`", "`glide.security.manager.deny`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"`glide.sm.default_mode`."},
{id:105,d:3,q:"An administrator sets `glide.sm.default_mode` to Deny Access. What is true afterwards?",opts:["The setting can be reverted to Allow Access at any time", "The setting cannot be reset to Allow Access once applied", "All operations including report_on and personalize_choices are restricted", "Only the admin role is affected"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Once set to Deny Access it cannot be reset to Allow Access. C is wrong — report_on and personalize_choices are explicitly unaffected."},
{id:106,d:3,q:"An ACL rule is created with the name `customer.*`. What does it secure?",opts:["The customer table record only, with no field access", "All rows and all fields on the customer table", "Only fields that have no explicit ACL", "Only the customer table's related lists"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"B is the best of the four, but be precise about what `table.*` actually is: `platform-security/access-control/acl-rule-types.md` lists it in the field ACL ordering — \"Match the table and any field (\\*). For example, `incident.*`\" — at position 4 of 6, *after* `table.field`, `parent.field` and `*.field`. So `customer.*` is the catch-all field rule for that table, not a record rule; the record rule is `customer` (shown as `.None`). ⚠️ Option C (\"only fields with no explicit ACL\") is closer to the runtime behaviour than it looks, because a more specific field rule matches first and \"the first successful field ACL evaluation stops ACL rule processing at the field level\" — but C is wrong as written, since a rule like `*.name` also outranks `customer.*`. Read the option set, not the wildcard alone."},
{id:107,d:3,q:"On table MyTable with field1, field2 and field3: `MyTable.None` grants read to admin and itil; `MyTable.field3` grants read to admin only. What can a user with only the itil role read?",opts:["field2 and field3", "field1 and field3", "field1, field2 and field3", "field1 and field2"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"itil passes the table rule, so field1 and field2 are readable; field3 carries its own admin-only rule that itil fails. ⚠️ One mined source keyed \"field1 and field3\" — wrong, field3 is precisely the restricted one."},
{id:108,d:3,q:"Which two methods are useful inside an Access Control script?",opts:["`gs.hasRole()`", "`current.isNewRecord()`", "`g_user.hasRole()`", "`g_form.getValue()`"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"ACL scripts are server-side: `gs.hasRole()` and `current.isNewRecord()`. `g_user` is client-side and `g_form` is a form API. Note the method is `isNewRecord()`, not `isNew()`."},
{id:109,d:3,q:"In a Business Rule, which call returns true if the currently logged-in user has the admin role?",opts:["`g_form.hasRoleExactly('admin')`", "`gs.hasRole('admin')`", "`g_form.hasRole('admin')`", "`gs.hasRoleExactly('admin')`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`gs.hasRole('admin')`. `gs.hasRoleExactly()` does not exist. The official Scripting Security reference splits the two APIs: client-side GlideUser (`g_user`) has `hasRole()`, `hasRoleExactly()`, `hasRoleFromList()`, `hasRoles()`; server-side GlideSystem (`gs`) has only `hasRole()` — plus `getUser()`, `getUserID()`, `getUserName()`, `isLoggedIn()`, `isInteractive()`, `getSession()`. So D is not a weaker answer, it is not a real method. A and C fail twice over: `g_form` is client-side *and* has no role methods at all."},
{id:110,d:3,q:"What is true of the client-side call `g_user.hasRole('x_my_app_user')`?",opts:["It returns true if the user has the x_my_app_user role or the admin role", "It returns false only if the user has the x_my_app_user role", "There is no `g_user.hasRole()` method", "It returns true only if the user has the x_my_app_user role"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"`g_user.hasRole()` returns true for the named role or admin, because admin is treated as holding every role. Q110/Q111 pair: `hasRole` includes admin, `hasRoleExactly` does not."},
{id:111,d:3,q:"Which method returns true only if the logged-in user has the catalog_admin role and no other role?",opts:["`g_user.hasRole('catalog_admin')`", "`g_user.hasRoleExactly('catalog_admin')`", "`g_user.hasRoleOnly('catalog_admin')`", "`g_user.hasRoleFromList('catalog_admin')`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`g_user.hasRoleExactly()` — true only when that is the user's sole role. ⚠️ Correction: an earlier version of this note claimed `hasRoleFromList` does not exist. It does — the official Scripting Security reference lists `hasRole()`, `hasRoleExactly()`, `hasRoleFromList()` and `hasRoles()` on `g_user`. `hasRoleFromList()` is real but answers a different question (does the user hold *any* of these roles). Only `hasRoleOnly` is invented."},
{id:112,d:3,q:"Which statement about client-side scripted security is correct?",opts:["Client-side scripts have access to both GlideSystem (gs) and GlideUser (g_user) methods", "Client-side scripts have access to the GlideUser (g_user) methods", "Client-side scripts have no access to user methods", "Client-side scripts have access to the GlideSystem (gs) user methods"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Client scripts get g_user only. `gs` is server-side."},
{id:113,d:3,q:"Which ACL operation enables users to execute client callable script includes and REST endpoints?",opts:["read", "write", "execute", "query_match"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"The `execute` operation covers client callable script includes and REST endpoint execution — which is why those artifact types appear in access-analysis questions (Q31)."},
{id:114,d:3,q:"When a custom table is created, which Access Control rules are automatically generated?",opts:["read", "create", "delete", "write", "execute"],ans:[0, 1, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"read, create, write, delete — four rules, one per CRUD operation. `execute` is not generated for a data table. ⚠️ An earlier draft of this bank keyed only three (omitting write); three independent sources key four, and a separate mined item phrases it as \"which four Access Controls are created\". Four is correct."},
{id:115,d:3,q:"During a pre-query ACL check, which parts of the ACL are evaluated?",opts:["Roles only", "Roles, conditions and scripts", "Conditions and scripts only", "Nothing; all checks happen post-query"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Pre-query checks roles only, because no record data exists yet. Roles, conditions and scripts are all evaluated post-query."},
{id:116,d:3,q:"A Deny-Unless ACL passes and no Allow-If ACL matches the request. What happens?",opts:["Access is denied because no Allow-If explicitly permitted it", "The system grants access by default", "The request is escalated to the table owner", "The Deny-Unless ACL is ignored"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Documented explicitly: if the Deny-Unless passes and no Allow-If matches, the system grants access by default. A is the intuitive-but-wrong reading."},
{id:117,d:3,q:"Which statement about a table with the \"Allow configuration\" Application Access option selected is true?",opts:["Only the in-scope application's scripts can create Business Rules for the table", "Any user with the application's user role can modify the application's scripts", "Out-of-scope applications can create Business Rules for the table", "Out-of-scope applications can add new tables to the scoped application"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"\"Allow configuration\" is design-time access — it lets out-of-scope applications create configuration records (such as Business Rules) against the table."},
{id:118,d:3,q:"For Application Access, the \"Allow access to this table via web services\" option is selected. Which statement is true?",opts:["Any user can now query the table through web services", "The user performing the query must still have the correct permissions to access the table's records", "It restricts access to SOAP only and does not apply to REST", "It permits reads but always blocks deletes"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Verbatim from the docs: the user must still have correct permissions even when the box is selected. The setting opens the door; ACLs still guard it."},
{id:119,d:3,q:"Which Application Access options become unavailable if \"Can read\" is NOT selected?",opts:["Access via web services", "Can create, Can update and Can delete", "None; Can read does not affect the other fields", "Allow configuration"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Can write, create and delete are each available only when Can read is selected — \"You must first select read access to grant any other API record operation.\" Web services access is independent, so A is the trap."},
{id:120,d:3,q:"What are the default runtime access permissions for a new application data table?",opts:["Accessible from is set to All application scopes", "Can read is enabled", "Can create, update and delete are all enabled", "Access via web services is disabled"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"docs",exp:"Default is Accessible from = All application scopes, Can read = Enabled, create/update/delete disabled, web services enabled. ⚠️ A widely-copied dump claims the baseline is \"only in-scope artifacts can read\" — the documentation says otherwise."},
{id:121,d:3,q:"Which records track cross-scope applications or scripts that request access to an application resource or event?",opts:["Restricted caller access records", "Caller tracking records", "Access control level records", "Cross-scope access records"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Restricted caller access `[sys_restricted_caller_access]` records. Distinguish from `sys_scope_privilege` (Q47), the runtime cross-scope privilege table."},
{id:122,d:3,q:"Which is NOT a valid Caller access field option?",opts:["Caller Tracking", "Caller Restriction", "None", "Caller Permission"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The options are Caller Tracking, Caller Restriction and None. \"Caller Permission\" does not exist."},
{id:123,d:3,q:"A developer hides a sensitive field using a Client Script and reports the field as secured. Why is that assessment wrong?",opts:["Client scripts run only for users with the admin role", "Client scripts affect only the browser and do not enforce server-side access", "Client scripts disable all ACLs on the table", "Client scripts run only after the record is deleted"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Browser-only. Same principle as Q89 — if it is not enforced server-side, it is not security."},
{id:124,d:3,q:"A module is restricted to the `x_app.user` role, but the table behind it has no read ACL for that role. What does the user experience?",opts:["Records open normally because the module role is sufficient", "The user reaches the list but cannot read any records", "The module role is copied automatically into a table ACL", "The table becomes readable by all application users"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Module roles govern navigation; ACLs govern data. `platform-security/access-control/r_ContextualSecurity.md` keeps them explicitly separate: \"Applications and modules contain lists of the roles required to view them… Security rights for applications and modules are still defined using role arrays\", while for data \"the system looks for ACL rules on fields and tables.\" So the role satisfies the module and gets the user to the list; the missing read ACL means no rows come back. C is the tempting wrong model — nothing copies a module role into an ACL. Internalise the split: a visible module is not an accessible record."},
{id:125,d:3,q:"When configuring a module, what does the \"Override application menu roles\" option do?",opts:["Users with the module role but without access to the application menu can access the module", "Self-Service users can access the module even without roles", "Admin is given access even if Access Controls would prevent it", "Users with application menu access see the module even without the module role"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"It lets users holding the module role in without the application-menu role."},
{id:126,d:3,q:"Which two database operations can be controlled with an Access Control?",opts:["Create", "Update", "Compile", "Index"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"Creating and updating records are both controllable; \"Compile\" and \"Index\" are not operations ACLs govern. But note the naming trap: the operation is called `write`, not \"update\" — `acl-rule-types.md` defines `write` as \"Enables users to update records in a table.\" If an option offers *write* and *update* side by side, write is the operation name. The full documented record-operation list is longer than most sources admit: `create`, `read`, `write`, `delete`, `execute`, `list_edit`, `report_on`, `report_view`, `add_to_list`, `personalize_choices`, `save_as_template`, `query_match`, `query_range`, `edit_task_relations`, `edit_ci_relations`."},
{id:127,d:3,q:"Which feature limits who can contribute to or read articles within a knowledge base?",opts:["Roles", "Groups", "Categories", "User Criteria"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"User Criteria governs knowledge base contribute/read. Roles and groups are the generic mechanisms; user criteria is the knowledge-specific one."},
{id:128,d:4,q:"Which is NOT a trigger type in Flow Designer?",opts:["Outbound Email", "Schedule", "Application", "Record"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Outbound Email is not a trigger. `build-workflows/workflow-studio/flow-triggers.md` documents five trigger categories — Record, REST, Scheduled, Application and Spoke. Sending outbound email is something a flow *does*, not something that starts it. Inbound email and inbound API calls *can* start a flow; outbound cannot."},
{id:129,d:4,q:"In Flow Designer, where is the output of an action stored so it can be used by subsequent actions?",opts:["Data Element", "Field Value", "Data Trigger", "Data Pill"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Action outputs, surfaced as data pills. `workflow-studio/actions.md` describes building actions by \"defining inputs and adding action steps\", with outputs then available downstream; `flow-triggers.md` has a whole section on \"Data pills available by trigger type\". The data pill is the *UI affordance*; the output is the thing it points at."},
{id:130,d:4,q:"What is a scoped application that contains Flow Designer actions tailored to a specific application or record type called?",opts:["Bundle", "Action", "Spoke", "Flow"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"A spoke. `flow-triggers.md` treats spokes as first-class — they even have their own trigger category, where \"Spokes can have conditional and event-driven external triggers or webhooks that start from third-party applications.\" A spoke is a scoped application bundling actions (and sometimes triggers) for one integration target."},
{id:131,d:4,q:"Which two ways can an application respond to an Event generated by `gs.eventQueue()`?",opts:["Script Action", "Scheduled Script Execution", "UI Policy", "Email Notification"],ans:[0, 3],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Script Actions and Email Notifications. `platform-administration/system-events/c_EventRegistry.md` names both: \"Registration lets other parts of the system, such as Email and SMS notifications and Script Actions, see the event in their list of available events and react to the event when it occurs.\" A Scheduled Script Execution runs on a clock and never subscribes to the queue — the recurring distractor here."},
{id:132,d:4,q:"Which object CANNOT be used in a Script Action script?",opts:["previous", "GlideRecord", "event", "current"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`previous`. A Script Action runs off the event queue, decoupled from the transaction that fired it, so there is no prior record state to compare. The closest documented statement is in `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md`, which says `previous` is \"Available on update and delete operations only. Not available on async operations.\" — the same decoupling. `current`, `event` and `GlideRecord` are all available."},
{id:133,d:4,q:"Which two objects are available in a Scheduled Script Execution script?",opts:["GlideSystem", "GlideRecord", "current", "g_form"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"GlideSystem and GlideRecord. A scheduled job has no triggering record, so there is no `current` — `platform-administration/time-configuration/c_ScheduledJobs.md` defines them as \"automated pieces of work that can be performed at a specific time or on a recurring schedule\", independent of any record. `g_form` is client-side. Pair with Q132: a Script Action has `current` and `event` but no `previous`; a Scheduled Job has neither `current` nor `previous`."},
{id:134,d:4,q:"Which four objects are available in an Inbound Email Action script?",opts:["`current`", "`event`", "`email`", "`logger`", "`previous`", "`producer`"],ans:[0, 1, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"docs",exp:"All four: `current` (the record the email refers to), `event` (`event.parm1`, `event.parm2`), `email` (`email.subject` — the inbound message), `logger` (writes to the email log). Straight from the script template in `platform-administration/t_CreatingAnInboundEmailAction.md`. ⚠️ This replaces a broken question. The mined version asked to pick one pair from \"current and email\" vs \"current and event\" — but the docs list both, so it had two correct answers and was unanswerable. `previous` belongs to Business Rules and `producer` to Record Producers."},
{id:135,d:4,q:"How does ServiceNow match an inbound email to an existing record?",opts:["sys_id in the body", "Watermark", "Subject line text", "Sender address"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The watermark. `platform-administration/c_EmailNotifications.md`: \"a unique watermark label will be generated at the bottom of each notification email to allow matching incoming email to existing records\", and `c_InboundEmailActions.md`: \"An inbound email action checks the email for a watermark that associates it with a task.\" Worth carrying: with no identifiable watermark the action *creates* a record; with the watermark of an existing record it *updates* that record."},
{id:136,d:4,q:"In an Email Notification, which syntax references a property of the event that triggered the notification?",opts:["`${event.<property name>}`", "`${current.<property name>}`", "`${<property name>.getDisplayValue()}`", "`${gs.<property name>}`"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"`${event.<property name>}` — the `event` object is what an event-triggered notification receives, so its parameters are reached through it. `${current.…}` reaches the *record*, which is a different object, and `gs` is not available in notification syntax at all. ⚠️ Not settleable from the local docs clone — the notification variable-substitution reference is not included. The answer is long-standing documented syntax and the distractors each name the wrong object, but treat the exact form as course-sourced."},
{id:137,d:4,q:"Regarding the Weight field on an Email Notification, which statement is NOT true?",opts:["The Weight value defaults to zero", "Only the highest-weight notifications for the same record and recipients are sent", "A Weight of zero means the notification is always sent when criteria are met", "A Weight of zero means no email should be sent"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The question asks which is NOT true. `platform-administration/c_EmailNotifications.md` covers the mechanism; the weight rule is that 0 means always send when criteria are met, and only the highest-weight notification for the same record and recipients goes out. So \"zero means no email\" is the false statement. ⚠️ Mined sources keyed this three different ways because the option order was scrambled — read the options, not the letter."},
{id:138,d:4,q:"Which three elements are configured in an Email Notification?",opts:["Who receives the notification", "The content of the notification", "When to send the notification", "Which database indexes to rebuild"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"`c_EmailNotifications.md` lists what an email notification lets an administrator specify: \"When to send the notification / Who receives the notification / What content is in the notification / Whether the notification can be delivered in an email digest.\" So there are actually four configurable elements — this item offers three of them plus an unrelated distractor. Compare Q290, which asks for all four."},
{id:139,d:4,q:"Which module is used to create a new email notification?",opts:["System Properties > Email > Settings", "User Preferences > Email > Notifications", "System Notification > Email > Notifications", "Administration > Notification Overview"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"System Notification > Email > Notifications. ⚠️ Not settleable from the local docs clone — `c_EmailNotifications.md` covers what a notification configures but not the navigation path. Menu paths are also the first thing to move between releases, so weight this one lightly: the *concept* (notifications are administered under System Notification, not System Properties or User Preferences) is what to carry, not the exact breadcrumb."},
{id:140,d:4,q:"Which two actions can a Business Rule take without any scripting?",opts:["Set field values", "Add a message", "Query an external REST endpoint", "Rebuild the table index"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Correct among the options offered, but the documented list is three, not two: `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` says \"Administrators can set field values, create information messages, and abort transactions without writing a script.\" Abort is the third no-script action; this item simply does not offer it. Cross-scope catch from the same page: on a table in a *different* scope, a before rule may use Set field values actions only — no scripts, and it cannot abort."},
{id:141,d:4,q:"Which Business Rule \"when\" option runs before the record is presented to the user and is used to populate `g_scratchpad`?",opts:["before", "after", "async", "display"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Display. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` timing table: a display rule \"Executed every time the corresponding form is displayed\" and is \"Used to make server-side objects available to client-side scripts\", writing to `g_scratchpad`. It runs *before the form is presented*, which is what the stem describes."},
{id:142,d:4,q:"Which object does a Display Business Rule NOT have access to?",opts:["previous", "GlideSystem", "g_scratchpad", "current"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`previous`. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md`: `previous` is \"Available on update and delete operations only. Not available on async operations.\" A display rule fires when a form is *requested* — nothing has been saved, so there is no prior version. ⚠️ One mined source keyed `g_scratchpad`, which is backwards: populating `g_scratchpad` is the display rule's signature use."},
{id:143,d:4,q:"For an Async Business Rule, which database operation CANNOT be configured as a trigger?",opts:["Query", "Insert", "Update", "Delete"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Query. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` states it flatly for async rules: \"Insert, Update, and Delete database operations. You cannot select Query.\" Async work is scheduled off a write; a read does not schedule anything."},
{id:144,d:4,q:"What happens when multiple Business Rules apply to the same table and operation?",opts:["They trigger in random order", "They trigger in ascending Order field sequence", "Only the first rule triggers", "They trigger simultaneously"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Ascending Order, lowest first. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` on the Order field: \"Enter a number indicating the sequence in which this business rule should run. If there are multiple rules on a particular activity, the rules run in the order specified here, from lowest to highest.\" Two gotchas from the same page: user-defined variables are globally scoped, so a variable declared in an order-100 rule is still visible to the order-200 rule that runs next; and for async rules \"the order of execution isn't guaranteed\" at all — use After rules or events when sequence matters."},
{id:145,d:4,q:"What is `g_scratchpad` used for?",opts:["To pass data from a client script back to the server", "To pass data from a server-side script to a client-side script when a form loads", "To store persistent data between user sessions", "To define constructors for a table class"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Server to client, on form load. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md`: a display rule is \"used to make server-side objects available to client-side scripts\", writing values to `g_scratchpad` for the client to read. It is one-way and one-shot — A inverts the direction (use GlideAjax for client-to-server) and C invents persistence across sessions."},
{id:146,d:4,q:"What is a benefit of creating an Application Properties page?",opts:["It provides a common landing page for an application", "It overrides application properties inherited from ServiceNow", "It tells users where to change the application's appearance", "It allows changes to application behavior without modifying application artifacts"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Change behaviour without modifying artifacts. That is the whole point of externalising configuration into `sys_properties`: an administrator changes the value, no script is edited or re-tested (compare Q276). A and C describe a homepage rather than a properties page. ⚠️ The properties-*page* module recipe itself is not in the docs clone — see Q147."},
{id:147,d:4,q:"Which Module Link type is used to access an Application Properties page?",opts:["URL (from Arguments)", "HTML (from Arguments)", "Script (from Arguments)", "Single Record"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"URL (from Arguments) — a documented Link type, used exactly this way in `platform-administration/platform-performance/t_CreateAConnectionTestModule.md` (\"Link type: URL (from Arguments)\"). The Arguments field carries `system_properties.do?sysparm_title=…&sysparm_category=…`. ⚠️ That argument string is not in the local docs clone — it comes from the course material, so treat the exact syntax as unverified. The discriminator is documented, though, and is the likely trap: a module that opens the *properties page* uses URL (from Arguments); a module that lists the property *records* uses List of Records with Table = System Properties [sys_properties] (`r_AvailableSystemProperties.md`). Read whether the stem says page or list."},
{id:148,d:4,q:"When creating a Utils Script Include, which step does NOT belong?",opts:["Identify the table", "Script the functions", "Create a class", "Create a prototype object from the new class"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"\"Identify the table\" does not belong. A Utils Script Include is table-agnostic reusable logic — `application-development/business-rules-and-script-includes.md`: \"Each Script Include defines either an object class or a function that can be reused among any server-side scripts.\" Nothing in that definition is bound to a table. The real steps are create a class, create a prototype object from it, and script the functions."},
{id:149,d:4,q:"Which two are benefits of storing server-side logic in a Script Include?",opts:["It runs only when called from a script", "It makes every script on the instance execute faster", "It gives one place to edit shared application logic", "It automatically exposes the logic to client scripts"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Runs only when called, and centralises shared logic. `business-rules-and-script-includes.md` recommends \"Use Script Includes instead of global Business Rules\" precisely because a global business rule loads on every transaction while a Script Include is inert until called, and it advises putting logic needed by a UI Action *and* a Scripted REST API into one Script Include called from both (Q288). D is false: client access additionally requires Client callable plus a GlideAjax call."},
{id:150,d:4,q:"What type of JavaScript function is the standard Business Rule script template?",opts:["Constructor", "Scoped", "Anonymous", "Self-invoking"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Self-invoking — the trailing `(current, previous)` is the distinguishing feature: `(function executeRule(current, previous) { … })(current, previous);`. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` explains *why* the wrapper exists: \"When this script is wrapped in a function, the variable is available only within the function and does not conflict with other functions using a variable named `now_GR`.\" That matters because business-rule variables are otherwise globally scoped across Order values. ⚠️ Strictly the template function is named (`executeRule`), so \"anonymous\" is the looser description — pick self-invoking."},
{id:151,d:4,q:"Which server-side object provides methods for working with dates in a privately scoped application?",opts:["GlideDateTime", "GlideRecord", "GlideSystem", "current"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"GlideDateTime, which has its own scoped API page (`api-reference/server-api-reference/c_GlideDateTimeScoped.md`) — the tell that it is available inside a privately scoped application. GlideRecord queries records, GlideSystem is the session/utility API, and `current` is a record reference, not a date API."},
{id:152,d:4,q:"In a Business Rule, which call returns the sys_id of the logged-in user?",opts:["`g_form.getUserID()`", "`g_form.getUserSysID()`", "`gs.getUserSysID()`", "`gs.getUserID()`"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`gs.getUserID()` — `api-reference/server-api-reference/c_GlideSystemScopedAPI.md`: \"Scoped GlideSystem - getUserID() — Gets the sys_id of the current user.\" ⚠️ Corrected: this bank previously keyed `gs.getUserSysID()` and called `getUserID()` a deprecated alternate. That was backwards — `getUserSysID()` does not exist at all. The complete `getUser*` set is `getUser()`, `getUserDisplayName()`, `getUserID()`, `getUserName()` (global scope adds `getUserNameByUserID()`). Same trap as `gs.hasRoleExactly()`: a plausible-looking method name that was never real. `g_form` is client-side and has no such method either."},
{id:153,d:4,q:"Which GlideSystem method returns the first and last name of the logged-in user concatenated together?",opts:["`gs.getUserName()`", "`gs.getUserID()`", "`gs.getUserDisplayName()`", "`gs.getUser()`"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`gs.getUserDisplayName()`. `api-reference/server-api-reference/c_GlideSystemScopedAPI.md` documents the trio precisely: `getUserDisplayName()` = the display name, `getUserName()` = \"the user name of the current user\" (the login), `getUserID()` = the sys_id. The login-vs-display-name confusion is the whole question — and note it is `getUserID()`, not the invented `getUserSysID()` (Q152)."},
{id:154,d:4,q:"Which feature tracks how long a task has been open to ensure it is completed within an allotted time?",opts:["Inactivity Monitor", "Business Time Remaining", "Service Level Agreements", "Response Time Clock"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Service Level Agreements. `it-service-management/service-level-management/exploring-slm.md`: Service Level Management covers \"a set of agreements between a service provider and customer that define the scope, quality and speed of the services being provided and ensuring that the agreed service levels are met.\" An SLA attaches a clock to a task and marks it breached if it runs past the agreed time. The other three option names are invented."},
{id:155,d:2,q:"Which testing framework is used to test ServiceNow applications?",opts:["Selenium", "Test Driven Framework (TDF)", "Automated Test Framework (ATF)", "JUnit"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Automated Test Framework."},
{id:156,d:2,q:"Which ATF test step sets up a specific user profile for testing purposes?",opts:["Impersonation", "Create a role", "Create a user", "Create a group"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The Impersonation step — confirmed in `automated-test-framework-atf/atf-use-basic-form.md`, whose worked example opens with \"Test step 1 - Impersonate\". ⚠️ A site advertising \"actual exam questions\" keyed *Create a user*; it is wrong. Creating a user makes a record; impersonation is what runs the test *as* that user."},
{id:157,d:4,q:"Which script always executes on the server side?",opts:["Business Rule", "UI Action", "Client Script", "UI Policy"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Business Rule. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` opens with the definition: \"A business rule is a server-side script that runs when a record is displayed, inserted, updated, or deleted, or when a table is queried.\" A UI Action can run either side — the Client check box decides — which is exactly why it is the intended distractor."},
{id:158,d:4,q:"What is a workflow context?",opts:["The checked-out workflow version being edited", "An instance generated from a workflow version that executes activities and follows transitions", "The table plus the conditions that start the workflow", "The business reason documented for the workflow design"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The running instance. `build-workflows/legacy-workflow/c_WorkflowVersions.md` draws the line where it matters: \"the changes are not applied to running workflow contexts. Any currently running workflow context continues using the workflow version that was available when the workflow started.\" Version = the design; context = one execution of it. That sentence is also the answer to Q320."},
{id:159,d:5,q:"What tool imports data from various data sources and maps that data into ServiceNow tables?",opts:["Update Set", "Transform Set", "Data Pack", "Import Set"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Import Set."},
{id:160,d:5,q:"Which platform feature determines the relationship between fields in an Import Set table and fields in the target table?",opts:["Business Service Management Map", "Data Source", "Transform Map", "Schema Map"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Transform Map. Distinguish the three: Data Source = where data comes from, Import Set table = the staging area, Transform Map = how staged fields land on the target."},
{id:161,d:5,q:"What are the three steps to import new data into ServiceNow from a spreadsheet?",opts:["Select Data Source, Schedule Transform, Review", "Load Data, Create Transform Map, Run Transform", "Define Data Source, Select Transform Map, Schedule Import", "Select Import Set, Create Data Pack, Run Transform"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Load Data → Create Transform Map → Run Transform. ⚠️ A mined source keyed \"Select Import Set, Select Transform Map, Run Transform\" — wrong; you cannot select an import set that does not exist yet. Loading the data is always step one."},
{id:162,d:5,q:"What is the purpose of the coalesce setting on a field map?",opts:["It merges two source columns into one target field", "It is the match field: if a record with that value exists it is updated, otherwise a new record is inserted", "It deletes target records that are absent from the source", "It converts the field's data type during transform"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Coalesce is the match field. Match found → update; no match → insert."},
{id:163,d:5,q:"A developer runs an import repeatedly with no coalesce field defined. What is the result?",opts:["Existing records are updated each time", "A new record is inserted on every run, creating duplicates", "The import fails with a validation error", "Only the first row is processed"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"With no coalesce field every row is treated as new, so each run inserts duplicates. This is the single most common real-world import mistake and a recurring exam item."},
{id:164,d:5,q:"When a ServiceNow instance requests information from an external web service, ServiceNow is acting as the web service:",opts:["Publisher", "Specialist", "Provider", "Consumer"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"ServiceNow is the consumer when it requests data. Consumer = calls out; provider = serves."},
{id:165,d:5,q:"When configuring a REST Message, the Endpoint is:",opts:["The command telling the REST script to stop execution", "The URI of the data to be accessed, queried, or modified", "Information about the format of the returned data", "The provider's response indicating there is no data to send back"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The URI of the data to be accessed."},
{id:166,d:5,q:"What is the REST API Explorer used for?",opts:["Practising REST interaction with public data providers", "Finding web resources for learning REST", "Converting SOAP methods to REST methods", "Creating sample code for ServiceNow REST requests"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`api-reference/rest-api-explorer/use-REST-API-Explorer.md`: it \"allows you to discover ServiceNow REST APIs, quickly construct and execute requests, and view responses… within your browser\", and generates the matching sample code. A is the tempting misread — it targets *ServiceNow's own* APIs, not third-party ones. Two extra facts: it needs the `rest_api_explorer` role, and per `api-reference.md` it is also how you *discover* which APIs exist before writing anything."},
{id:167,d:5,q:"In a SOAP Message, which syntax indicates a variable to be passed when the function is called?",opts:["`current.variable_name`", "`${variable_name}`", "`<variable_name>`", "`<variable_name>.do?WSDL`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"`${variable_name}`. ⚠️ Not settleable from the local docs clone — the classic SOAP/REST Message variable-substitution page is not included in it. The answer is the long-standing documented syntax and the three distractors are each clearly wrong (`current.x` is server script, `<x>` is XML markup, `.do?WSDL` is the WSDL endpoint), so it is kept — but treat the syntax as sourced from course material rather than from this clone."},
{id:168,d:5,q:"Which HTTP method would an Outbound REST Message use to retrieve data without modifying it?",opts:["GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"GET is the read-only verb."},
{id:169,d:5,q:"A developer needs to expose a custom ServiceNow endpoint that external systems can call with a custom URL and custom logic. What should they build?",opts:["An Outbound REST Message", "A Scripted REST API", "A Transform Map", "An Import Set"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A Scripted REST API — custom endpoint, custom logic. An Outbound REST Message goes the other direction."},
{id:170,d:5,q:"Which two statements distinguish inbound from outbound integration?",opts:["Inbound means an external system calls ServiceNow", "Outbound means ServiceNow calls an external system", "Inbound means ServiceNow calls an external system", "Outbound integrations are configured with Import Sets"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Inbound = they call us; outbound = we call them. Anchor it to Q164: consumer/outbound is ServiceNow reaching out."},
{id:171,d:5,q:"Which ServiceNow feature includes data from a secondary, related table on a report?",opts:["Joins", "SQL", "Dot walking", "Outer join"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Dot walking reaches through a reference field to pull in fields from the related table."},
{id:172,d:5,q:"Which statement about a Data Source record is correct?",opts:["It defines where the import data comes from, such as a file, JDBC connection, or attachment", "It maps import fields to target fields", "It stores the transformed records", "It schedules the ACL evaluation for imported records"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"The Data Source defines the origin — file, JDBC, attachment, REST."},
{id:173,d:5,q:"During a transform, which script runs before each row is transformed?",opts:["onBefore", "onAfter", "onStart", "onComplete"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"onBefore runs per row before the transform; onAfter runs per row after. onStart/onComplete bracket the whole transform."},
{id:174,d:6,q:"Which of the following is NOT a way to install an application on a ServiceNow instance?",opts:["Install an application from the Application Repository", "Select the Copy button on the application record", "Download and install an application from ServiceNow Share", "Download and install a third-party application from the ServiceNow Store"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"There is no Copy button that installs an application. The real routes are documented in `application-development/c_SharingApplications.md` (publish to the application repository, the ServiceNow Store, an Update Set, or push to team development instances) and `application-repository-self-hosted/install-app-from-repo.md` (All > System Applications > My Company Applications → pick a version → Install, role `admin`). ServiceNow Share is the community site, distinct from the certified Store (Q229)."},
{id:175,d:6,q:"Where can an application be published once it is ready to share?",opts:["To the ServiceNow Store", "To a local drive", "To an application repository", "To an Update Set", "To a spreadsheet"],ans:[0, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"`c_SharingApplications.md` tabulates the sharing methods and who each reaches — worth learning as a table, because the *audience* is what gets tested: application repository → \"all instances assigned to the same company\"; ServiceNow Store → \"all ServiceNow customers\"; Update Set → \"any instance with access to the Update Set file\", for compliance or backup. ⚠️ There is a fourth method this question omits: push to team development instances, for pushing developer changes to the parent instance. Local drives and spreadsheets are not publication targets."},
{id:176,d:6,q:"What is the ServiceNow Application Repository?",opts:["A database for logging application errors", "A database for tracking user permissions", "A database containing custom ServiceNow applications", "A collection of files in a Git database"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"A database containing custom applications. `application-repository-self-hosted/app-repo.md`: \"a central repository for all scoped applications that are published by all ServiceNow customers.\" ⚠️ Correcting an earlier oversimplification here: the Repository is not a rival to the Store — `manage-apps.md` says \"The Application Repository is part of the ServiceNow Store\", with storage partitioned so \"customers cannot see or access other customers' published applications.\" So: same infrastructure, your org's apps only. D is the trap — a Git repository is a different thing entirely."},
{id:177,d:6,q:"How can you see which artifacts will be included in a published application?",opts:["Enter the application name in Global search", "Open the Update Sets list", "Examine the Application Files related list on the application record", "Open every artifact record individually"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The Application Files related list. `c_ApplicationFiles.md`: \"Application developers create application files when they add configuration records for application logic such as business rules, workflows, and script includes… Application developers can view the complete list of application files from the custom application record.\" Note the ownership subtlety in the same line: a scoped app does not own the `sys_script` table, but it does own its records in it."},
{id:178,d:6,q:"What contains the configuration changes made in an instance and helps move those changes from Dev to another environment?",opts:["Import sets", "Update sets", "Transform maps", "System dictionaries"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Update sets. Contrast with the Application Repository (Q62), which distributes versioned applications rather than moving config between environments."},
{id:179,d:6,q:"What is required to link a ServiceNow application to a Git repository?",opts:["User name", "URI", "Password", "ACL", "URL", "Application name"],ans:[0, 2, 4],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Best of the options offered, and Application name is not required — that is the distractor, and also the answer to the inverted \"which is NOT required\" phrasing. \"URI\" is a near-miss for \"URL\". ⚠️ The current dialog is framed differently from this question. `servicenow-studio-classic/t_LinkAnApplicationToSourceControl.md` lists the fields as Network protocol (HTTPS or SSH), URL, Credential, Branch, MID Server Name, Default email, Commit Comment — the username/password pair now lives inside a Credential record (Basic Auth for HTTPS, SSH private key for SSH), not as loose fields. Two facts worth carrying: an access token can replace the password, and \"all application developers on the instance share a single set of repository credentials.\""},
{id:180,d:6,q:"How can an application link to a repository that sits behind a firewall?",opts:["This option is not supported", "Link the application to source control through a MID Server", "Link the application to source control through an access token", "Link the application to source control with multi-factor authentication"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Through a MID Server, stated outright in `t_LinkAnApplicationToSourceControl.md`: \"Linking or importing an application through a MID Server enables access to repositories behind a firewall.\" Git traffic is packaged as a `.bundle` file and relayed via the MID Server (`tips-source-control-midserver.md`). Trap on C: access tokens are real — the docs recommend one *instead of a password* — but a token does not solve a network-reachability problem."},
{id:181,d:6,q:"What is the source control operation that stores local changes so they can be applied later?",opts:["Branch", "Tag", "Stash", "Update set"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Stash. `servicenow-studio-classic/r_AvailableSourceControlOperations.md` defines Stash Local Changes as \"Removes and saves local changes for later work\", with Manage Stashes to \"apply or delete stashed changes\". A *branch* is a parallel line of development and a *tag* marks a point in history — neither shelves work in progress. Bonus fact from the same table: only Create Branch and Create Tag are available from *both* Studio and the Git repository; everything else listed is Studio-only or repository-only."},
{id:182,d:6,q:"You are developing MyApp, which has Table A. You want Table A's records installed as part of the application. Table A's records will be installed when:",opts:["Table A is active and extends the Task table", "Table A's records are added to the application record using the Create Application Files feature", "Table A has an automatic number counter for new records", "Table A is not in the System Clone > Exclude Tables list"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Create Application Files. `t_IncludeApplicationData.md`: \"The system can export selected records as application files that are included as part of the application update set when you share it\", reached from the list's column context menu (List v2) or list title menu (List v3). Three things worth carrying: the export is a snapshot — \"the application data only includes the version of the records that existed when the records were shared… The system does not update this snapshot when the records change\"; you set Load When (for example *New Install and Upgrades*); and it is not meant for bulk data movement — use Import from another instance for that."},
{id:183,d:6,q:"Which statement about Delegated Development is INCORRECT?",opts:["Administrators can specify which application file types a developer may access", "Administrators can grant access to script fields", "Administrators can grant access to security records", "Administrators can grant non-admin users the ability to develop global applications"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Confirmed against the docs: \"Developer permissions are available only for scoped apps, not global apps\" (`delegated-development-and-deployment/t_AddADeveloper.md`). So granting non-admins the ability to develop *global* applications is the incorrect statement. The other three are documented controls. A dump keyed A; it is wrong."},
{id:184,d:6,q:"What is displayed to delegated developers so they can package changes for deployment in ServiceNow Studio?",opts:["Tools tab", "Update Set Picker", "Export to XML", "Publish button"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The Publish button."},
{id:185,d:6,q:"Which statement is true for the Application Picker?",opts:["All custom application scopes and the Global scope appear in the Application Picker", "All applications including baseline applications like Incident appear in the Application Picker", "Only custom applications appear in the Application Picker", "Only downloaded applications appear in the Application Picker"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"All custom scopes plus Global. `application-development/c_ApplicationPicker.md`: the picker opens \"a list of application scopes that you have access to\" — scopes, not applications. Incident lives *in* the Global scope rather than being a scope of its own, which is why B fails."},
{id:186,d:6,q:"How does the Application Picker interact with Application Scope?",opts:["Global is reserved and does not appear in the picker", "Selecting Global sets the Application Scope to Incident", "Selecting an application does not set the Application Scope", "Selecting an application sets the Application Scope"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Selecting an application sets the Application Scope. Same doc: the picker \"enables application developers to view and select the application scope where their changes apply\", and `c_ApplicationContext.md` adds that new records are \"automatically assign[ed] to the currently selected application in the application picker\". Concrete tell worth memorising: when the scope is set to anything non-Global, the picker icon shows a red ring."},
{id:187,d:6,q:"Which two features are available to Global applications?",opts:["Automated Test Framework", "Flow Designer", "Delegated Development", "Application scope protection policies"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"ATF and Flow Designer work with global applications. Delegated Development is documented as scoped-only — \"Developer permissions are available only for scoped apps, not global apps\" (`t_AddADeveloper.md`) — and scope protection policies are scoped by definition, so C and D are both out."},
{id:188,d:6,q:"Which business requirements should be documented as part of the application development plan?",opts:["Data input and output", "Business problem", "Project schedule", "Process steps", "Database capacity", "Users and stakeholders", "Available licenses"],ans:[0, 1, 3, 5],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"Business problem, data in/out, process steps, users/stakeholders. Project schedule, database capacity and licensing are project-management concerns, not application design inputs."},
{id:189,d:6,q:"Which is NOT a purpose of application scoping?",opts:["Provide a relationship between application artifacts", "Provide a way of tracking which user developed an application", "Provide a namespace to prevent cross-application name collisions", "Provide controls for how scripts from another scope can alter tables in a scoped application"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Scoping does not track who developed an application. It provides namespacing, artifact relationships and cross-scope controls."},
{id:190,d:4,q:"Which module would you use to create a new automation of business logic such as approvals, tasks, and notifications?",opts:["Process Automation > Workflow Editor", "Process Automation > Active Flows", "Process Automation > Process Flow", "Process Automation > Flow Designer", "Process Automation > Flow Administration"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"⚠️ Corrected — the module has been renamed. Current documentation navigates to All > Process Automation > Workflow Studio (`workflow-studio/actions-cust-spk.md`), and `workflow-studio/actions.md` states that \"Workflow Studio is the default ServiceNow AI Platform process automation builder… Workflow Studio replaces the Workflow Editor.\" Flows are still flows and the flow-building surface is still commonly called Flow Designer, but the module you open is Workflow Studio. Older material — and this question's original wording — says Flow Designer; if both appear as options, prefer Workflow Studio for a current exam."},
{id:191,d:4,q:"Where do Business Rules execute in the ServiceNow architecture?",opts:["Client-side only", "Server-side only", "Both client and server-side", "Neither client nor server-side"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Server-side only. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md`: \"A business rule is a server-side script…\", and its breadcrumb files it under *Server-side scripting*. Contrast Q157: a UI Action can run on either side."},
{id:192,d:4,q:"Which are types of Business Rule based on when they execute in the transaction lifecycle?",opts:["Before insert and Before update", "After insert and After update", "Display", "All of the above"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"All of the above. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` lists the *when* values as before, after, async and display — so before insert/update, after insert/update and display are all genuine types. A question offering \"All of the above\" where each listed item is independently valid is usually keying it. Note the one the question omits: async."},
{id:193,d:4,q:"Which two logging methods are available in a privately scoped application?",opts:["`gs.log()`", "`gs.info()`", "`gs.error()`", "`gs.print()`"],ans:[1, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Scoped applications use `gs.info()`, `gs.warn()`, `gs.error()` and `gs.debug()`. ⚠️ `gs.log()` is global-scope only and is the trap here — one mined source keyed `gs.log()`, which is wrong for a *privately scoped* app. `gs.print()` does not exist."},
{id:194,d:4,q:"Which is NOT supported by Flow Designer?",opts:["Calling a subflow from a flow", "Testing a flow with rollback", "Using a Delegated Developer", "Running a flow from a MetricBase Trigger"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Testing does not roll back. The docs say it outright: \"Because testing a flow creates or changes records on the instance, flow designers should always test flows on a non-production instance\" (`build-workflows/workflow-studio/flow-test.md`). ⚠️ Corrected — this bank previously keyed D. MetricBase *is* a supported trigger and has its own page (`create-mb-flow.md`), though it needs a separate subscription; `delegated_developer` appears throughout Workflow Studio; subflows are core. Two dump sites split between C and D, and the one this bank followed was the wrong one."},
{id:195,d:3,q:"What setting allows users to view a Knowledge Base article even if they are not logged in?",opts:["The ESS role", "The Allow All role", "The Public setting", "The View All setting"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The Public setting. Pairs with Q127: *User Criteria* controls who can read/contribute within a knowledge base for logged-in users; *Public* is what exposes an article to anonymous visitors."},
{id:196,d:1,q:"Which ServiceNow utility provides a modern interactive graphical interface to visualise configuration items and their relationships?",opts:["Class Map", "Flow Design", "Dependency View", "Business Service Map"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Dependency View — configuration items and their relationships."},
{id:197,d:1,q:"What do you click when you have made modifications to a report and want to see the results without saving?",opts:["Execute", "Preview", "Run", "Test"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Preview renders the modified report without committing the change."},
{id:198,d:1,q:"What provides a graphical view of the relationships among tables?",opts:["Schema map", "Dependency view", "Business Service Map", "Map source report"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Schema map — *table* relationships in the data model. Q196/Q198 are the pair that catches people: both stems say \"graphical view of relationships\", so read whether it says configuration items (dependency view) or tables (schema map)."},
{id:199,d:1,q:"Which is the base table of the configuration management database hierarchy?",opts:["`cmdb_rel_ci`", "`ucmdb`", "`cmdb_ci`", "`cmdb`"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"`cmdb_ci` is the base configuration-item class that every CI table extends. `cmdb_rel_ci` stores CI *relationships*, and `ucmdb` is a different vendor's product."},
{id:200,d:4,q:"What role does the trigger play in a flow?",opts:["It specifies where the flow should execute", "It specifies what the flow should execute", "It specifies when the flow should execute", "It specifies why the flow should execute"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Trigger = when. `build-workflows/workflow-studio/exploring-flows.md`: \"When the flow trigger conditions are met, the flow runs a sequence of reusable actions and flow logic to complete the process.\" Q200/Q201 are a deliberate pair — trigger answers *when*, action answers *what*. Memorise the pairing, not the sentence."},
{id:201,d:4,q:"What is an action responsible for in a flow?",opts:["Specifying when the flow should execute", "Specifying why the flow should execute", "Specifying where the flow should execute", "Specifying what the flow should execute"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Action = what. `build-workflows/workflow-studio/exploring-flows.md`: once triggered, \"the flow runs a sequence of reusable actions and flow logic to complete the process\" — the actions are the work performed. Pairs with Q200."},
{id:202,d:4,q:"What is the main purpose of flow logic?",opts:["It allows the system scheduler to determine execution timing", "It provides users manual time selection for flow launch", "It is a programming structure for decisions and logical choices", "It enables developers to keep flows organised"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"The decision and branching structure. `build-workflows/workflow-studio/exploring-flows.md` sets flow logic alongside actions as the other half of a flow, and the shipped constructs are exactly branching and looping primitives — `If`, `Else`, `For Each`, `Do Until`, `Try`, `Wait for a duration`, `Do the following in parallel`, `Make a decision`, `Exit loop`, `Set flow variables`. Trap on D: keeping flows tidy is what annotations do; logic changes what actually runs."},
{id:203,d:4,q:"Which three are valid Flow Designer trigger types?",opts:["Record-based", "Notification-based", "Application-based", "Schedule-based", "Condition-based"],ans:[0, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Correct among the options offered — \"Notification-based\" and \"Condition-based\" are invented; a condition is part of a *record* trigger, not a type of its own. ⚠️ But do not memorise \"the three trigger types\". `build-workflows/workflow-studio/flow-triggers.md` documents five categories: Record, REST (needs an IntegrationHub Enterprise subscription), Scheduled, Application, and Spoke (webhooks from third-party systems). If an exam item offers REST or Spoke, they are real."},
{id:204,d:4,q:"On a record trigger, which Run Trigger option fires the flow on every update, even if a context for that flow is already running?",opts:["Only if not currently running", "Once", "For every update", "For each unique change"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"For every update — \"every time that the record is updated, regardless of whether there has already been or currently are any running contexts\". The current four options are *For each unique change*, *Once*, *Only if not currently running*, *For every update*. ⚠️ This replaces a broken question. The mined version offered Always, which the docs say is the *previous-release name* for Only if not currently running — so two of its four options were the same thing under different names. If a stale exam item offers \"Always\", it means today's *Only if not currently running*."},
{id:205,d:4,q:"Which three are components of an action?",opts:["Transitions", "Outputs", "Inputs", "Activities", "Steps"],ans:[1, 2, 4],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Inputs, Outputs, Steps. `workflow-studio/actions.md`: \"Create and edit actions by defining inputs and adding action steps\", and publishing preserves \"their current action steps, variables, and sequence\". Transitions and Activities belong to legacy Workflow — the same page says \"Workflow Studio replaces the Workflow Editor\", which is exactly the trap. Two related facts: Core actions cannot be viewed or edited from the Workflow Studio interface, and spoke actions are read-only but can be copied and customised."},
{id:206,d:4,q:"What are action outputs?",opts:["Data variables representing the results of the action", "Data variables the action requires to begin processing", "Data variables used only within the action", "Data variables available to a parent flow after a subflow completes"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Outputs are the action's results. `workflow-studio/actions.md` describes authoring an action by \"defining inputs and adding action steps\", then testing to \"review the runtime values they generate\" — those generated values are the outputs, surfaced downstream as data pills. B describes inputs; D describes *subflow* outputs (Q209), which is the trap."},
{id:207,d:4,q:"What occurs when an action is added to a flow?",opts:["The data pane is emptied", "The data pill picker selects fields via reference lookup", "Data pills are created to capture runtime variables", "The end user receives an email notification"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Data pills are created. `workflow-studio/flow-triggers.md` has a section titled \"Data pills available by trigger type\", and the same model applies to actions: adding one publishes its runtime values as pills for later steps to consume. See Q129 — the pill is the UI affordance, the action output is the underlying value."},
{id:208,d:4,q:"What is used to pass data between a flow and a subflow?",opts:["Inputs and outputs", "Activities", "An action", "The scratchpad"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Inputs and outputs — the same mechanism `workflow-studio/actions.md` describes for actions, applied at subflow level. Trap on D: `g_scratchpad` is a *form* mechanism carrying server data to client scripts (Q145/Q289) and has nothing to do with flows. Cross-topic distractors like this are common — check the artifact the object actually belongs to."},
{id:209,d:4,q:"What are subflow inputs responsible for?",opts:["The timing details of subflow execution", "Specifying the data available to the subflow when it launches", "The execution details of subflow operations", "Specifying the data available to the parent flow after the subflow completes"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Inputs specify what the subflow receives at launch. Same inputs/outputs model as actions (`workflow-studio/actions.md`), applied at subflow level: inputs go in when it starts, outputs come back to the parent when it finishes — which is option D, and the deliberate near-miss. `flow-logic-assign-subflow-outputs.md` exists precisely to bind those returned outputs."},
{id:210,d:4,q:"Which two are characteristics of a subflow?",opts:["It provides customers with self-service opportunities", "It contains inputs and outputs that pass data to and from the subflow", "It sequences reusable actions that can be started from a flow, subflow, or script", "It organises configuration activities into categories"],ans:[1, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Inputs/outputs, and a reusable sequence callable from a flow, subflow or script. `workflow-studio/flow-logic-assign-subflow-outputs.md` and the actions model cover the data contract; reusability is the design point — a subflow is built once and called from several places, the same argument as putting shared logic in a Script Include instead of duplicating it (Q288)."},
{id:211,d:4,q:"Which step is NOT performed when a flow is processed?",opts:["The system creates an entry in the event queue", "The system builds a process plan from the flow", "The system stores execution details in the syslog table", "The system runs the process plan using the triggering record"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Execution details do not go to `syslog` — they live on the flow context record. `workflow-studio/add-worknote-link.md` describes adding \"a link to the current flow context record. Use the link to view the flow execution details of the current flow.\" ⚠️ The other three options use ADF course wording (\"builds a process plan\") that does not appear in the docs clone; the *answer* is settled, the distractors' phrasing is course-sourced."},
{id:212,d:4,q:"Which two roles grant full access to all Flow Designer features?",opts:["admin", "flow_operator", "flow_designer", "workflow_admin"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"`admin` and `flow_designer`, named together in `workflow-studio/getting-started-dynamic-flow-get-flow-outputs.md`: \"In the Roles field, enter `flow_designer` or `admin`\". `workflow_admin` belongs to legacy Workflow."},
{id:213,d:4,q:"Which three are reasons to create Flow Designer content in a scoped application rather than in Global?",opts:["Scoped apps can be shared via the application repository", "Scope helps categorise content for maintenance", "Scope protects applications from damage by others", "Scope limits access to Flow Designer itself"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Repository sharing, maintenance categorisation, and protection — all three are genuine scoping benefits. `application-development/c_SharingApplications.md` confirms the first (publishing to the application repository reaches \"all instances assigned to the same company\"), and `c_ApplicationScope.md` covers namespacing and cross-scope protection. D is false: scoping governs what artifacts can reach each other, not who may open Flow Designer — that is role-based (Q212, `admin` / `flow_designer`)."},
{id:214,d:4,q:"Which is NOT an example of when an application would use a Scheduled Script Execution?",opts:["The application needs to send weekly email reminders for all records on a table", "The application needs to run a clean-up script on the last day of every month", "The application needs to query the database every day for unassigned records", "The application needs to run a client-side script at the same time every day"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"You cannot schedule a client-side script. `platform-administration/time-configuration/c_ScheduledJobs.md` defines scheduled jobs as server-side \"automated pieces of work that can be performed at a specific time or on a recurring schedule\" — reports, record generation from templates, and recurring scripts. A browser-side script needs a browser with the form open, so there is nothing for the scheduler to run."},
{id:215,d:4,q:"What is the Event Registry?",opts:["A table containing a record for every Event known to the system, which allows ServiceNow to react when Events are generated", "A workflow launched every time an Event is generated, used to debug Events", "The method used in server-side scripts to generate Events and pass parameters", "The Event Log listing every Event that has been generated"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"A table of every event the system recognises. `platform-administration/system-events/c_EventRegistry.md`: \"The events registry lists the events the system recognizes. Use registered events to automate other activities, such as script actions or notifications.\" Registration is what makes an event visible to notifications and Script Actions (Q279/Q280). C describes `gs.eventQueue()`; D describes the Event Log."},
{id:216,d:4,q:"Which three script types execute on the server?",opts:["Business Rule", "Client Script", "UI Policy", "Script Action", "Scheduled Job"],ans:[0, 3, 4],multi:true,src:"web+docs",exp:"Business Rule, Script Action, Scheduled Job. `api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` defines a business rule as server-side; `c_EventRegistry.md` puts Script Actions on the server reacting to queued events; `c_ScheduledJobs.md` puts scheduled work on the server. Client Scripts and UI Policies run in the browser. The reliable tell is `gs` — if the artifact can use GlideSystem, it is server-side."},
{id:217,d:3,q:"A table has three fields: field1, field2, field3. The Access Control list is: `table.None` read for admin and itil; `table.*` read for admin only; `table.field3` read for itil. Which field or fields can a user with only the itil role read?",opts:["field1, field2 and field3", "field3 only", "field1 and field3", "All fields except field3"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"field3 only. Work it field by field: field1 and field2 have no field-specific rule, so they fall back to `table.*` — which is admin only, so itil fails. field3 has its own itil rule, so it passes. ⚠️ Compare Q107, which looks almost identical but has no `table.*` rule and therefore answers \"field1 and field2\". The presence or absence of the wildcard row flips the answer completely — read the ACL list, not the shape of the question."},
{id:218,d:3,q:"How must Application Access be configured to prevent all other private application scopes from creating configuration records on an application's data tables?",opts:["Create Access Controls instead; Application Access cannot do this", "Set Accessible from to All application scopes and clear Can create", "Set Accessible from to This application scope only and clear the web services option", "Set Accessible from to This application scope only"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Accessible from = This application scope only. That single setting closes design-time access from every other scope; you do not also need to touch web services (C) and clearing Can create (B) does not stop *configuration* records."},
{id:219,d:3,q:"A customer requires that Incident numbers are read-only on all lists and forms for all users, and that Short description is mandatory on insert across all applications. Which type of policy meets this?",opts:["Dictionary Design Policy", "Field Criteria Policy", "Data Quality Policy", "Data Policy"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Data Policy — the giveaway is \"across all applications\" and \"on insert\", i.e. enforcement independent of the form. A UI Policy could not satisfy either clause. Reinforces Q69."},
{id:220,d:3,q:"Which tab on a knowledge base record identifies the sets of users able to read articles in that knowledge base?",opts:["Access List", "Can Access", "Accessible to", "Can Read"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The Can Read tab."},
{id:221,d:5,q:"What is the purpose of the coalesce field when importing data?",opts:["When a match is found, a new record is inserted", "To determine whether a record matches an existing record or is a new record", "If a match is not found, the existing record is updated", "To identify and merge duplicate records"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Coalesce = the match test. A and C state the behaviour backwards (match → update, no match → insert), which is exactly how this item is made hard. See Q162/Q163."},
{id:222,d:5,q:"Which of the following is NOT a method used by the ServiceNow REST API?",opts:["COPY", "POST", "GET", "DELETE"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"COPY is not a REST verb here. GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE are."},
{id:223,d:5,q:"Application developers can specify which page a user sees after submitting a record through a Record Producer. How is that page specified?",opts:["An after Business Rule on the target table setting `window.redirect`", "An application property storing the URL", "A script in the Record Producer's Script field setting `producer.redirect = \"<URL>\"`", "Configuration on the module that opens the Record Producer"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"`producer.redirect = \"<URL>\";` in the Record Producer's Script field. ⚠️ The mined source keyed \"an application property\" — that is wrong; a property stores a value, it does not perform a redirect. Note this uses the same `producer` object as Q78."},
{id:224,d:5,q:"Which one allows the creation of a task-based record from the Service Catalog?",opts:["UI Builder", "Flow Designer", "Assignment Rule", "Record Producer"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Record Producer — a catalog front end that creates a task-based record. See Q76."},
{id:225,d:2,q:"When designing a form, what do you create to organise fields on the form?",opts:["Related lists", "Tabs", "Sections", "Buttons"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Sections. Tabs are how sections are *displayed*, but the thing you create is a section."},
{id:226,d:2,q:"What is the name of the string that displays the filter criteria on a list?",opts:["Breadcrumb", "Choice", "Menu", "Topic"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"The breadcrumb shows and lets you unwind filter conditions."},
{id:227,d:1,q:"On a form, which field type shows an icon that can be clicked to preview the associated record?",opts:["Reference", "Lookup", "Quickview", "Drilldown"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Reference fields carry the preview (ⓘ) icon. \"Lookup\", \"Quickview\" and \"Drilldown\" are not field types."},
{id:228,d:1,q:"Which three tables are available by default in a ServiceNow instance?",opts:["Task", "Item", "User", "Incident", "Project"],ans:[0, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"Task, User (`sys_user`) and Incident ship with the platform. \"Item\" and \"Project\" are not baseline tables in this sense."},
{id:229,d:6,q:"What is the ServiceNow Store?",opts:["The source for ServiceNow Community created developer content", "A marketplace for free and paid certified ServiceNow applications and integrations", "A downloadable ServiceNow script archive", "An alternate name for the ServiceNow Developer Share site"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"A marketplace for certified apps and integrations. `c_SharingApplications.md` gives the discriminator by audience: the Store reaches \"all ServiceNow customers\" and is for \"share or sell applications to other companies\", whereas the application repository reaches \"all instances assigned to the same company\". A and D describe Share, the community site. Q174/Q176/Q229 are a trio — sort them by *who can see it*."},
{id:230,d:6,q:"What is the best practice regarding the Default update set for moving customisations between instances?",opts:["Submit the Default update set to the application repository", "You should not use the Default update set for moving between instances", "Keep the Default update set to a maximum of 20 records for troubleshooting", "Merge Default update sets before moving between instances"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"web+docs",exp:"Never move changes with the Default update set. `system-update-sets/default-update-sets.md` is blunt: \"The global default set provides system functionality and shouldn't be changed, deleted, or moved between systems.\" Related facts from the same page: \"Only one update set can be the default set for any application scope\", and marking a default set Ignored or Completed makes the system immediately auto-generate a replacement for that scope."},
{id:231,d:1,q:"The Task table is an example of which two of the following?",opts:["Legacy class", "Child class", "Base class", "Parent class"],ans:[2, 3],multi:true,src:"web",exp:"Task is the base class of the task hierarchy and the parent of Incident, Problem, Change and so on. It is never a child — nothing sits above it."},
{id:232,d:1,q:"Tables that extend another table do what?",opts:["Sometimes inherit the parent's fields", "Automatically update the application scope", "Do not inherit the parent's fields", "Inherit the parent's fields"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Extension inherits the parent's fields — unconditionally, not \"sometimes\". Reinforces Q42/Q55."},
{id:233,d:6,q:"What do you install when you want to add applications or functionality within your development instance?",opts:["Patch", "Update Pack", "App Package", "Plugin"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"A Plugin. ⚠️ The source keyed this as \"Patch, Update Pack, App Package\" — that is wrong, and a good example of why these keys need checking. Patches and update packs are *upgrade* artifacts; plugins are what you activate to add functionality."},
{id:234,d:1,q:"Which one of the following is NOT true for Modules?",opts:["Access to Modules is controlled with roles", "Modules open content pages", "Every Module must be associated with a table", "Every Module must be part of an Application Menu"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"A module need not be tied to a table — it can open a URL, a content page, or run a script. ⚠️ One source keyed D instead (\"must be part of an Application Menu\"), but modules do live under an application menu, so D is a true statement and cannot be the answer to a NOT question."},
{id:235,d:2,q:"You are looking at a list of Active Incidents and want to exclude Incidents with the State of Resolved. How might you do that?",opts:["On the State column title, right-click and select Filter Out > Resolved", "On the list of records, locate and right-click the Resolved value, then select Exclude", "Click the funnel icon, click AND, select Resolved, Is Not, State, then click Run", "On the list of records, locate and right-click the Resolved value, then select Filter Out"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"web",exp:"Right-click the value in the list and choose Filter Out. A is wrong because you right-click the value, not the column title; B invents an \"Exclude\" item; C has the condition operands in the wrong order."},
{id:236,d:2,q:"Which method is valid for `GlideUser()`?",opts:["`getUserName()`", "`getFullName()`", "`getRole()`", "`getRoleExactly()`"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"`getFullName()` is a real GlideUser method. Trap: `hasRole()` and `hasRoleExactly()` exist, but `getRole()` / `getRoleExactly()` do not — the verb is `has`, not `get`. And the login name is the property `g_user.userName`, not a `getUserName()` method. Compare Q110/Q111."},
{id:237,d:4,q:"How do you configure a Scheduled Job to execute on the last day of every month?",opts:["Set the Run field value to Periodically and the Repeat Interval value to 31", "Set the Run field value to Periodically and the Repeat Interval value to Last Day", "Set the Run field value to Monthly and the Day field value to 31", "Set the Run field value to Monthly and the Day field value to Last Day"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Monthly + Day 31. ServiceNow clamps the day to the end of shorter months, so 31 means \"last day\" for every month. Trap: D reads more naturally in English, but the Day field takes a number — there is no \"Last Day\" value. Periodically counts a fixed interval and drifts against calendar months."},
{id:238,d:6,q:"When managing global application files, which action is allowed?",opts:["Moving application files into or out of the scoped application", "Adding files from global scope to a global application", "Changing the scope of a global application", "Removing an application's scope"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"docs",exp:"Global-to-global file moves are permitted. The other three are all things scope exists to prevent: you cannot drag files across a scope boundary (A), cannot re-scope an existing application (C), and cannot strip an application's scope (D). Useful anchor — scope is assigned at creation and is effectively permanent."},
{id:239,d:5,q:"Which utility determines whether field names in an Import Set match the field names on the target table?",opts:["Auto Map Matching Fields", "Schema Map", "Mapping Assist", "Field Watcher"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Auto Map Matching Fields matches source columns to target fields by name. This item appears in the official sample list but ServiceNow published it without its options, so the options here are authored. Schema Map shows table relationships (Q198); Mapping Assist is the manual drag-and-drop mapper; Field Watcher is a debugging tool."},
{id:240,d:5,q:"Which two statements about the Import Set table are correct?",opts:["It is a staging table that holds the raw imported rows", "It is the final destination of the imported data", "Its rows are transformed onto the target table by a Transform Map", "It must be created manually before every import"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"The import set table is a staging table; a Transform Map moves its rows to the target. It is created automatically by the load, so D is false, and it is never the final destination (B)."},
{id:241,d:5,q:"During a transform, which script runs once before the entire transform begins?",opts:["onStart", "onBefore", "onAfter", "onComplete"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"onStart runs once at the beginning; onComplete once at the end. onBefore/onAfter run per row (Q173). Learn the pair: Start/Complete bracket the run, Before/After bracket each row."},
{id:242,d:5,q:"A Transform Map field map needs to derive the target value from several source columns. What should be used?",opts:["A coalesce field", "A source script on the field map", "A Data Policy", "A Business Rule on the import set table"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A source script on the field map computes the value. Coalesce is for matching, not deriving."},
{id:243,d:5,q:"Two field maps on the same Transform Map are both marked as coalesce. What is the effect?",opts:["Only the first coalesce field is used", "The import fails validation", "A record matches only when both values match", "A record matches when either value matches"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Multiple coalesce fields form a composite key — all must match for the row to be treated as an update. A common real-world surprise: adding a second coalesce field makes matching *stricter*, so previously-updating rows start inserting."},
{id:244,d:5,q:"Which Data Source format options are available for a file-based import?",opts:["CSV", "Excel (XLSX)", "DOCX", "PPTX"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"CSV and Excel. Word and PowerPoint are not import formats."},
{id:245,d:5,q:"A scheduled import needs to pull a file from an external server behind the customer's firewall. What is required?",opts:["A MID Server", "A Scripted REST API", "An Outbound REST Message", "A Data Policy"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"A MID Server reaches resources behind the firewall — the same answer as the source-control case in Q180. If a question says \"behind a firewall\", the answer is almost always MID Server."},
{id:246,d:5,q:"Which statement about the `sys_import_set_row` table is correct?",opts:["It stores the Transform Map definitions", "It stores the raw staged rows for an import set", "It stores the transformed target records", "It stores the data source connection details"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`sys_import_set_row` holds the staged rows."},
{id:247,d:5,q:"In an Outbound REST Message, where do you define a value that will be supplied at call time?",opts:["A variable substitution such as `${record_number}`", "A coalesce field", "A data policy", "A UI policy"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Variable substitution `${...}` in the endpoint or body, supplied at call time. Same syntax family as the SOAP case in Q167."},
{id:248,d:5,q:"Which two are true of a Scripted REST API?",opts:["It defines a custom inbound endpoint that external systems call", "It is used by ServiceNow to call an external system", "Each resource specifies an HTTP method and a relative path", "It replaces the need for authentication"],ans:[0, 2],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Scripted REST API = inbound, with resources defined by HTTP method plus relative path. B describes an Outbound REST Message — the direction is the whole distinction (Q169/Q170). D is never true of anything."},
{id:249,d:5,q:"Which HTTP status code family indicates the request succeeded?",opts:["2xx", "3xx", "4xx", "5xx"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"2xx = success, 3xx = redirect, 4xx = client error, 5xx = server error."},
{id:250,d:5,q:"A REST call from ServiceNow returns HTTP 401. What does that indicate?",opts:["The endpoint does not exist", "Authentication failed or was not supplied", "The server encountered an internal error", "The request was malformed"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"401 = unauthorised, i.e. credentials missing or rejected. 404 is \"does not exist\", 500 is server error, 400 is malformed. Worth knowing 401 vs 403: 401 means \"who are you?\", 403 means \"I know who you are and you still can't.\""},
{id:251,d:5,q:"Which object is used in a script to send an outbound REST request in a scoped application?",opts:["`sn_ws.RESTMessageV2`", "`GlideRecord`", "`GlideAjax`", "`sn_import.ImportSet`"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"`sn_ws.RESTMessageV2` is the scoped outbound REST API. The `sn_ws` namespace is the tell."},
{id:252,d:5,q:"After running a transform, where do you look to see which source rows failed and why?",opts:["The Import Set Run history and the transform log", "The System Log > Emails", "The Update Set list", "The Schema Map"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"The Import Set Run history plus the transform log record row-level errors."},
{id:253,d:5,q:"Which two are valid ways to bring spreadsheet data into ServiceNow?",opts:["Load Data from an attached CSV or Excel file", "Create a Data Source pointing to a file, then schedule the import", "Paste the rows into a UI Page", "Attach the file to an Update Set"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"authored",exp:"Load Data (ad hoc) or a Data Source plus schedule (repeatable). Update sets carry configuration, not data (Q178)."},
{id:254,d:5,q:"What does the Run Transform step actually do?",opts:["Uploads the file into the import set table", "Applies the Transform Map to move staged rows onto the target table", "Deletes the import set table", "Publishes the application"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"authored",exp:"Run Transform applies the map from staging to target — the third of the three steps in Q161."},
{id:255,d:2,q:"According to ServiceNow's guidance, how do UI policies compare with client scripts for showing, hiding, or requiring a field?",opts:["Client scripts load faster, so they are preferred", "Both can perform these actions, but UI policies are preferred because they load faster", "Only client scripts can make a field mandatory", "UI policies run only on form load; client scripts run on change"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-administration/t_CreateAUIPolicy.md` states both halves: UI policies \"dynamically change the behavior of information on a form\", and \"You can also use client scripts to perform all of these actions, but for faster load times use UI policies when possible.\" The capability overlaps; the tie-breaker is performance. Trap on A: the preference runs the other way."},
{id:256,d:2,q:"A UI policy's condition references a field that is not on the form layout. What happens?",opts:["The condition is skipped because the field is not on the form", "The condition still evaluates — UI policy conditions evaluate all fields whether or not they appear on the form", "The UI policy deactivates itself", "The form raises a client-side error"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`t_CreateAUIPolicy.md`: \"A UI policy condition evaluates all fields even if they are not visible on the form. This function removes the requirement that a field must be on a form for it to be evaluated.\" The field does not need to be on the layout."},
{id:257,d:2,q:"Which role is required to create a UI policy?",opts:["ui_policy_admin", "personalize_form", "ui_admin", "form_admin"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`ui_policy_admin`, stated under Before you begin in `t_CreateAUIPolicy.md`. The others are invented. Navigation is All > System UI > UI Policies."},
{id:258,d:2,q:"A UI policy needs to do something more complex than making fields visible, mandatory, or read-only. What do you use?",opts:["Convert it to a data policy", "Select the Run scripts option on the UI policy", "Replace it with an access control", "Add a Business Rule on the same table"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`t_CreateAUIPolicy.md`: \"Basic UI policies do not require any scripting, however for more advanced actions, use the Run scripts option.\" Note the knock-on for Q259 — turning Run scripts on makes the policy ineligible for conversion to a data policy."},
{id:259,d:2,q:"Which three conditions must hold before a UI policy can be converted to a data policy?",opts:["The Run scripts check box must be cleared", "The Global check box must be selected", "Every UI policy action must have Visible set to Leave Alone", "The UI policy must already be inactive", "The policy must affect exactly one field", "The table must be in the global scope"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"`t_ConvertAUIPolicyToADataPolicy.md` lists exactly these three: Run scripts cleared, Global selected, and no action with Visible set to True or False — Visible must be Leave Alone. The policy does not need to be inactive first."},
{id:260,d:2,q:"What happens to the UI policy when you convert it to a data policy?",opts:["Nothing — both stay active independently", "The UI policy is deactivated; to keep enforcing it on the form, select Use as UI Policy on client on the data policy", "The UI policy is deleted", "The new data policy is created read-only"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Converting a UI policy to a data policy deactivates the UI policy. To retain the policy in the UI, ensure that the Use as UI Policy on client check box is selected on the data policy record.\" An easy mark lost by assuming both keep running."},
{id:261,d:2,q:"Why choose a data policy over a UI policy to make a field mandatory?",opts:["Data policies render faster on the form", "A data policy also applies to import sets and data arriving via SOAP web services, not just the form", "Data policies can run client-side scripts", "Data policies apply only to the desktop UI"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"You can also apply a UI policy to import sets or to data imported by SOAP web services when you convert it to a data policy.\" The discrimination: a UI policy guards the form; a data policy guards the data whichever route it arrives by."},
{id:262,d:2,q:"Where are UI policies not supported?",opts:["Forms in the Content Management System", "Search screens", "Lists in the Content Management System", "Forms on extended tables"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`t_CreateAUIPolicy.md`: \"UI policies are not supported on search screens.\" The same note rules out the distractors — \"UI Policies also apply to forms and lists displayed within Content Management System application.\""},
{id:263,d:2,q:"What does a record producer create?",opts:["A requested item in the Service Catalog", "A task-based record, such as an incident", "An import set row", "A catalog item definition"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`servicenow-platform/service-catalog/c_RecordProducer.md`: \"A record producer is a specific type of catalog item that allows end users to create task-based records, such as incident records, from the service catalog... the record producer generates a task record such as incident, instead of a requested item.\""},
{id:264,d:2,q:"A developer wants users to raise a requested item through the catalog. Should they build a record producer for it?",opts:["Yes — record producers are the standard way to create requested items", "No — record producers should create task-based records only; use a catalog item so standard catalog processes such as workflows run as expected", "Yes, provided the table is in the same scope", "No, requested items can only be created by an import"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc, stated as a rule: \"Use a record producer to create task-based records only\" and \"To ensure that standard service catalog processes are followed, such as initiating workflows as expected, do not create requested item records from record producers. Instead, create requested item using catalog items.\""},
{id:265,d:2,q:"For which tables can you create a record producer?",opts:["Tables and database views in the same scope as the record producer", "Tables in other scopes that allow create access from other applications", "Any table on the instance, regardless of scope or access", "Only tables that extend Task in the global scope"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"You can create a record producer for tables and database views that are in the same scope as the record producer. You can also create a record producer for tables that allow create access from applications in other scopes.\" C ignores scope; D invents a Task-only limit."},
{id:266,d:2,q:"Which of these should a record producer script avoid?",opts:["Calling current.update()", "Reading values from the producer object", "Setting field values on current", "Redirecting the user after submission"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Do not call the update, setAbortAction method, or set the sys_class_name on current record to avoid unexpected behavior.\" Setting fields and redirecting are the record producer's normal job. Related fact: if the script aborts generation, no row is written to Item Produced Record [sc_item_produced_record]."},
{id:267,d:2,q:"An automated test creates records while it runs. What happens to that data when the test finishes?",opts:["It stays on the instance and must be removed manually", "The system rolls the data back once all steps in the test complete", "It is copied to an archive table", "It is committed only if every step passed"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`automated-test-framework-atf/atf-run-test.md`: \"If your test creates data, the system rolls back that data after all steps in the test complete.\""},
{id:268,d:2,q:"How does running an ATF test differ from testing a flow in Flow Designer?",opts:["ATF rolls back the data it creates; testing a flow creates or changes records on the instance", "Testing a flow rolls back; ATF does not", "Neither one creates records", "Both roll back automatically"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"The pair to memorise. ATF rolls back (`atf-run-test.md`); flow testing does not — `build-workflows/workflow-studio/flow-test.md` says \"Because testing a flow creates or changes records on the instance, flow designers should always test flows on a non-production instance.\" Both advise a sub-production instance, but only one cleans up after itself."},
{id:269,d:2,q:"Where should automated tests be run?",opts:["On a non-production instance", "On production, outside business hours", "On any instance, because ATF is read-only", "Only inside a scoped application"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`atf-run-test.md` says it twice, including in the page description: \"After creating an automated test, run it on a non-production instance.\" Rollback covers data the test creates, not every side effect it triggers."},
{id:270,d:2,q:"Which ATF feature lets you pause a test part-way through a run in order to troubleshoot it?",opts:["Breakpoints", "Step templates", "Parameterized data", "Test suites"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`atf-breakpoints-rollback.md`: \"Breakpoints allow you to pause your test at any step of a test run in order to troubleshoot and test authoring.\""},
{id:271,d:2,q:"Several tests need the same opening sequence of steps. What saves rebuilding it each time?",opts:["Copy the whole test and rename it", "Add a predefined list of steps — a template — to the test", "Move the steps into a Script Include", "Group the tests into a test suite"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`atf-build-overview.md`: \"Some steps frequently occur in the same sequence in many different tests, so you can add a predefined list of steps (template) to an automated test.\" Trap on D: a test suite groups whole tests; it does not share steps between them."},
{id:272,d:2,q:"Which two are true of automated test steps?",opts:["Data can be passed from one test step to another", "Form steps can produce results screenshots", "Each step is isolated and cannot see anything an earlier step did", "Test steps can only run server-side"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"`atf-build-overview.md` names both: \"Passing data from one automated test step to another\", and for tests involving form steps, \"View results screenshots from an automated test.\" C contradicts A directly."},
{id:273,d:4,q:"Which method reads a system property in a scoped application and returns a fallback when the property does not exist?",opts:["gs.getProperty(key, alt)", "gs.getValue(key, alt)", "gs.property(key, alt)", "gs.readProperty(key, alt)"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`api-reference/server-api-reference/c_GlideSystemScopedAPI.md` documents `getProperty(String key, Object alt)` — \"Gets the value of a Glide property. If the property is not found, returns an alternate value.\" The other three method names do not exist."},
{id:274,d:4,q:"Which call sets a property's value together with a description?",opts:["gs.setProperty(key, value, description)", "gs.putProperty(key, value, description)", "gs.property.set(key, value, description)", "gs.updateProperty(key, value, description)"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same reference, shown in its own example: `gs.setProperty(\"glide.foo\",\"bar\",\"foo\")` — key, value, description."},
{id:275,d:4,q:"Which table stores system properties?",opts:["sys_properties", "sys_config", "sys_app_property", "sys_settings"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`sys_properties`. Confirmed by the configuration reference in `platform-security/instance-security-hardening-settings/sc-enable-report-view-acls.md`, which gives Configuration type as \"System Properties (/sys_properties_list.do)\"."},
{id:276,d:4,q:"Why store a configurable value as an application property instead of hard-coding it in a script?",opts:["Properties are encrypted at rest", "An administrator can change the value without editing the script", "Properties are evaluated faster than variables", "Properties are the only way to share a value between scopes"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"⚠️ The one v2 item not settled by a documentation quote. The docs clone covers the property *API* (`c_GlideSystemScopedAPI.md`) and the `sys_properties` table, but has no page stating why you would prefer a property over a hard-coded value. B is the standard rationale — configuration separated from code, changeable without editing and re-testing a script. Answer it by elimination rather than by trusting B on its own: ordinary properties are not encrypted (A), and cross-scope sharing is governed by application access and cross-scope privileges, not properties (D)."},
{id:277,d:4,q:"What type does gs.getProperty() return?",opts:["String", "Boolean", "GlideRecord", "Whatever type the property was declared as"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_GlideSystemScopedAPI.md` gives the return type as String — \"The value of the Glide property, or the alternate object defined above.\" This is the fact behind Q278."},
{id:278,d:4,q:"A property holds the text \"false\". A script runs `if (gs.getProperty('my.flag')) { ... }`. What happens?",opts:["The block is skipped, because the value is false", "The block runs, because the returned value is the non-empty string \"false\", which is truthy", "The script throws a type error", "The block runs only in the global scope"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Follows directly from Q277: the call returns the string \"false\", and every non-empty string is truthy in JavaScript, so the block runs. This is the most common application-property bug. Compare explicitly, e.g. `gs.getProperty('my.flag') === 'true'`."},
{id:279,d:4,q:"You have created a new event and a business rule that fires it. What must you do so notifications and script actions can respond to it?",opts:["Register the event in the event registry", "Restart the instance", "Attach the event to a scheduled job", "Nothing — events are discovered automatically"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-administration/system-events/c_EventRegistry.md`: \"After you create a new event and a business rule that uses the event, you must register it.\""},
{id:280,d:4,q:"What does registering an event make possible?",opts:["Email and SMS notifications can see it in their list of available events", "Script Actions can react to it when it occurs", "The event is written to the syslog table automatically", "The event runs immediately instead of being queued"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Registration lets other parts of the system, such as Email and SMS notifications and Script Actions, see the event in their list of available events and react to the event when it occurs.\" This is also why a Scheduled Script Execution is never the answer to \"what consumes an event\" — it runs on a clock, it does not subscribe."},
{id:281,d:4,q:"Which of these is NOT something a scheduled job is used for?",opts:["Generating and distributing a report automatically", "Generating records from a template on a recurring schedule", "Running a script at the end of the month", "Reacting to a record the moment a user saves it"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-administration/time-configuration/c_ScheduledJobs.md` defines scheduled jobs as \"automated pieces of work that can be performed at a specific time or on a recurring schedule\" and lists A, B and C among the tasks they automate. Reacting the instant a record is saved is a Business Rule's job — that is the discrimination being tested."},
{id:282,d:4,q:"A scheduled job has been added to the scheduler queue and is waiting to run. Which state is it in?",opts:["Ready", "Queued", "Running", "Error"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Queued — \"Job has been added to the scheduler queue and is waiting to run\" (`c_ScheduledJobs.md`). Paired deliberately with Q283; Ready and Queued are the two that get swapped."},
{id:283,d:4,q:"Which scheduled job state means the job will run at its next scheduled interval?",opts:["Ready", "Queued", "Running", "Waiting"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Ready — same doc as Q282: \"Job is ready to run at the next scheduled interval.\" The four documented states are Ready, Running, Queued and Error; \"Waiting\" is invented."},
{id:284,d:4,q:"Where do you monitor both event processing and scheduled job processing?",opts:["The System Events and Jobs Dashboard", "The System Log", "The flow execution list", "The Transaction Log"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_ScheduledJobs.md`: \"Use the System Events and Jobs Dashboard to monitor the system event processing system and the scheduled jobs processing system.\" One dashboard covers both."},
{id:285,d:4,q:"What does a Script Include define?",opts:["Either an object class or a function, reusable by server-side scripts", "A client-side library loaded on every form", "A recurring unit of scheduled work", "A REST endpoint"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`application-development/business-rules-and-script-includes.md`: \"Each Script Include defines either an object class or a function that can be reused among any server-side scripts.\""},
{id:286,d:4,q:"Which recommendation appears in ServiceNow's guidance on Business Rules?",opts:["Use global Business Rules instead of Script Includes", "Use Script Includes instead of global Business Rules", "Run Business Rules without conditions so they never miss a change", "Finish every Business Rule with current.update()"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc's good-practice list includes \"Use Script Includes instead of global Business Rules\" and \"Always use a condition with Business Rules\" — which also rules out C (\"Business Rules rarely run with no conditions\")."},
{id:287,d:4,q:"Which of these should be avoided inside a Business Rule?",opts:["current.update()", "Setting a field on current in a before rule", "Using a condition to control when the rule runs", "Calling a Script Include"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"current.update() should not be used in any Business Rules. Using current.update() triggers an additional database operation, which could cause duplicate notifications, recursive loops, etc.\" Setting a field on current in a *before* rule is the documented normal pattern, so B is correct practice."},
{id:288,d:4,q:"The same logic is needed by a UI Action and by a Scripted REST API. What is the recommended structure?",opts:["Call the Business Rule from the UI Action", "Put the logic in a Script Include and call it from both", "Duplicate the code in each place", "Call the UI Action from the Scripted REST API"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc, almost verbatim: \"Instead of calling a Business Rule from a UI Action or a UI Action from a Scripted REST API, put the code in a Script Include and call the Script Include from both places.\" It also notes this lets you test the function before deploying it elsewhere."},
{id:289,d:4,q:"Which Business Rule type is used to make server-side objects available to client-side scripts through g_scratchpad?",opts:["Before", "After", "Async", "Display"],ans:[3],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Display. Same doc's Business Rule timing table: Display runs \"every time the corresponding form is displayed\" and is \"used to make server-side objects available to client-side scripts\", with the worked example writing to `g_scratchpad`. Compare Q142 — a display rule has no `previous` object, because nothing has been saved yet."},
{id:290,d:4,q:"Which four things does an email notification let an administrator specify?",opts:["When to send the notification", "Who receives it", "What content it contains", "Whether it can be delivered in an email digest", "Which mail server relays it", "The recipient's time zone"],ans:[0, 1, 2, 3],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-administration/c_EmailNotifications.md` lists exactly these four: when to send it, who receives it, what content it holds, and whether it can go in an email digest. Mail relay and recipient time zone are not part of the notification record."},
{id:291,d:4,q:"Which format do new email notifications use by default?",opts:["Rich HTML", "Plain text", "Raw HTML source", "Markdown"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"The rich HTML format is the default for all new email notifications.\""},
{id:292,d:4,q:"What does the rich HTML format do to links that point at instance records?",opts:["Converts relative URLs to absolute links so they do not break", "Shortens them to save space", "Strips them for security", "Forces them to open in a new tab"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"To avoid broken links, items like images and incidents, that are linked with URLs relative to an instance are converted to absolute links.\""},
{id:293,d:4,q:"An email notification can also go out as an SMS. What does the system use as the SMS text by default?",opts:["The subject line of the email notification", "The first line of the email body", "The name of the notification record", "A separate SMS-only notification record"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"The system uses the subject line of the email notification and converts it to an SMS message.\" An administrator can override this with an alternate SMS message on the email template or notification form."},
{id:294,d:2,q:"A developer states that GlideRecord cannot be used in a client script at all. Is that correct?",opts:["Correct — GlideRecord is server-side only, and a client script cannot reference it", "Incorrect — a client-side GlideRecord API exists, though GlideAjax is preferred for performance", "Correct — client scripts must use GlideForm for every data operation", "Incorrect — client-side GlideRecord is the recommended way to query the server from a form"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"A client-side GlideRecord API exists. `api-reference/api-client.md` lists \"GlideRecord - Client — The GlideRecord API provides methods that perform database operations. This API enables the use of some GlideRecord functionality in client-side scripts, such as client scripts and UI policy scripts.\" Note *some* — it is a reduced surface. The discrimination that actually matters: it exists, but a synchronous client-side query blocks form rendering, so GlideAjax plus a client-callable Script Include is the recommended route. D overshoots in the other direction by calling it *recommended*. This bank previously asserted A and was wrong — see Q97."},
{id:295,d:2,q:"Which client-side API is documented as deprecated?",opts:["GlideForm", "GlideAjax", "GlideDialogWindow", "GlideUser"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"GlideDialogWindow. `api-reference/api-client.md` labels it in the listing itself: \"GlideDialogWindow - Client \\(deprecated\\)\". GlideForm, GlideAjax and GlideUser carry no such marker."},
{id:296,d:1,q:"Which three questions does ServiceNow tell you to ask before deciding to build a new application?",opts:["Could you configure or customize an existing application instead?", "How many people will use it, and how often?", "Are you willing to maintain it as things change over time?", "Does the process already exist in another vendor's product?", "Can the application be built without any scripting?", "Will the application need more than one table?"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"`determining-good-candidates-for-apps.md` lists exactly these three: \"Can you configure or customize an existing application instead of building a new one?\", \"How many people will the application serve, and how often would they use it? If not many people would use it frequently, is it worth the effort?\", and \"Are you willing to maintain the application as things change over time?\" D, E and F are plausible engineering questions that the guidance never asks."},
{id:297,d:1,q:"A team can meet its requirement by customizing an out-of-the-box application. What does ServiceNow warn is the consequence of doing so?",opts:["Customizations are lost at every family release upgrade", "Customizing can complicate upgrades, and you own the code you customize and are responsible for maintaining it", "Customizing an out-of-the-box application is not permitted on a production instance", "Customizations cannot be captured in an update set"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Configuring and customizing existing applications can lead to complications with upgrading to a new version of the ServiceNow AI Platform. If you customize your applications, you own that code and are responsible for its upkeep.\" Trap on A: customizations are not *lost* on upgrade — that overstates it; the problem is the maintenance burden and upgrade friction you take on."},
{id:298,d:1,q:"Which three are given as good reasons to build a new application rather than customize an existing one?",opts:["A net-new process that does not fit neatly into any existing module", "A use case that could be built by customizing an out-of-the-box app but does not align with that app's intent", "A process that is unique or proprietary, or spans multiple departments", "A requirement that involves more than five tables", "A process that only administrators will ever use", "A requirement to store data outside the ServiceNow platform"],ans:[0, 1, 2],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc, under \"Examples of when to create apps\": Net-new process (\"a new process that doesn't fit neatly into any of the existing modules\"), Misaligned intent (\"could be built by customizing an OOTB app. However, it doesn't align with the app intent\"), and Separate group workflow (\"unique, proprietary, or spans multiple departments\"). Table count and audience are never the test."},
{id:299,d:1,q:"Besides upgrade risk, which two consequences of building a custom application does the documentation tell you to factor in?",opts:["Licensing and cost implications, which should be discussed with your admin", "Customer service and tech support may not be able to support custom code, because they do not know the expected functionality", "Custom applications cannot be published to the Application Repository", "Custom applications are excluded from instance cloning"],ans:[0, 1],multi:true,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Creating apps in ServiceNow can have licensing and cost implications. Talk with your admin\", and \"Customer service and tech support may not be able to support custom code and applications because they don't know the expected functionality.\" C and D are invented restrictions."},
{id:300,d:1,q:"A request comes in for an application that a handful of people would use once or twice a year, and which an existing application could already handle with minor configuration. Applying the documented guidance, what is the right call?",opts:["Build it as a scoped application, because scoping isolates it from everything else", "Configure the existing application instead — the usage does not justify the build and maintenance effort", "Build it in the global scope so it needs no cross-scope configuration", "Build it, because configuring an existing application always breaks upgrades"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc as Q296 (`determining-good-candidates-for-apps.md`). Two of its three screening questions point the same way here: an existing application can be configured, and the user base is small and infrequent — \"If not many people would use it frequently, is it worth the effort?\" Trap on D: the doc says customizing \"can lead to complications with upgrading\", not that it *always breaks* upgrades — an absolute where the source is a caution."},
{id:301,d:3,q:"How is access to an application menu and its modules controlled?",opts:["Each carries a list of the roles required to view it", "By an access control on the `sys_app_application` table", "By the application's scope alone", "By a UI policy on the navigator"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-security/access-control/r_ContextualSecurity.md`: \"Applications and modules contain lists of the roles required to view them. For example, to view the System Definition application, the admin role is required. Security rights for applications and modules are still defined using role arrays.\" Note this is navigation-level visibility, not data security — the table's access controls still decide what the user can actually read (compare Q124)."},
{id:302,d:3,q:"A user holds the role listed on a module, but not the role listed on the application menu above it. By default, can they see the module?",opts:["Yes — a module role always overrides the application menu", "No — by default the user must have the roles on both the application menu and the module", "Yes, but only if the module opens a list rather than a form", "No, and there is no way to change this behaviour"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Default behaviour requires both. `platform-administration/time-configuration/t_MakeATimelineVisibleToASelUser.md` describes the Override application menu roles check box as making the module \"appear when the user has the specified roles. Otherwise, the user must have the roles specified by both the application menu and the module.\" So D is wrong — that check box is exactly how you change it (Q125)."},
{id:303,d:4,q:"Why must a legacy workflow be checked out before it can be edited?",opts:["So that changes apply only to the user who checked it out, while everyone else keeps using the published version", "So that the workflow can be moved into a different application scope", "So that the workflow's activities can be run in debug mode", "So that the workflow can be attached to an update set"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`build-workflows/legacy-workflow/c_WorkflowVersions.md`: \"workflows must be checked out before they can be edited… When a workflow is checked out, changes apply only to the user who has the workflow checked out. Other users can continue to use the published workflow.\" Check-out is about isolating edits, not scope, debugging or update sets."},
{id:304,d:4,q:"How many users can have the same legacy workflow checked out at once?",opts:["One", "Two — an editor and a reviewer", "Any number, with changes merged on publish", "Any number, but only the last publish is kept"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Only one user can check out a workflow at a time.\""},
{id:305,d:4,q:"A new version of a workflow is published while several workflow contexts are still running. What happens to those running contexts?",opts:["They are cancelled and must be restarted", "They immediately switch to the new version at their next activity", "They continue on the version that was current when they started; the next run uses the new version", "They pause until an administrator migrates them"],ans:[2],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"When a new version of an existing workflow is published, the changes are not applied to running workflow contexts. Any currently running workflow context continues using the workflow version that was available when the workflow started. The next time the workflow runs, it uses the updated, published version.\" This is the version-vs-context distinction in its most testable form."},
{id:306,d:4,q:"How is a legacy workflow's application scope determined?",opts:["It inherits the scope selected in the application picker when it is created, and cannot be changed in the Workflow Editor", "It is set on the workflow's Properties tab and can be changed at any time", "It always runs in the global scope", "It inherits the scope of whichever record triggers it"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_WorkflowScope.md`: \"When a workflow is created, it inherits the application scope from the gear menu for the logged in user. This scope cannot be changed in the Workflow Editor.\" It then runs in that scope, and is callable from another application only if its accessibility permits all scopes."},
{id:307,d:4,q:"A privately scoped workflow calls out to common global resources such as approvals, events and SLA timers. What does the documentation say happens?",opts:["The calls succeed, because these resources are always accessible to all scopes", "The calls fail — with an exception, or a hung activity waiting for results that never return", "The workflow is automatically promoted to public scope", "The calls succeed but are logged as cross-scope violations"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"any privately scoped workflows that make calls out to other scoped resources fail with either an exception or a hung activity while waiting for returned results\", naming ECC queues, Tasks, Approvals, Events and SLA timers. The hung-activity failure mode is the nastier one — nothing errors, the workflow just stops advancing."},
{id:308,d:4,q:"Which statement about custom activities and workflow scope is correct?",opts:["A custom activity always runs in the scope of the workflow that contains it", "A custom activity runs in its own scope, which may differ from the workflow's", "Custom activities cannot be used in a privately scoped workflow", "Custom activities uploaded to the ServiceNow Store must be private"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Same doc: \"Custom activities run in their own scope, even if it is different from that of the workflow.\" D is inverted — custom activities uploaded to the Store \"must be configured as accessible to all application scopes\". C is wrong too: private activities can be used inside a public workflow, and are protected in both directions."},
{id:309,d:4,q:"A workflow runs on the Requested Item [sc_req_item] table. What is true of its stage field?",opts:["It is set automatically to the table's Stage field and cannot be changed", "Any choice field on the table can be selected as the stage field", "Stages are unavailable on Requested Item workflows", "The stage field must be created manually before stages can be used"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_WorkflowStages.md`: \"For workflows that use the Requested Item [sc_req_item] table, the stage field is automatically set to the Stage field of the table and cannot be changed.\" For other tables you choose the stage field in workflow properties, and only fields on that table are available — which is what makes B wrong as a general claim."},
{id:310,d:4,q:"What is the current status of legacy workflows relative to Workflow Studio?",opts:["Legacy workflows and Workflow Studio receive new features in parallel", "Legacy workflow functionality has been replaced by Workflow Studio content, and only Workflow Studio receives new features and enhancements", "Legacy workflows have been removed entirely and existing ones no longer run", "Workflow Studio is a preview feature; legacy workflow remains the supported option"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_WorkflowConcepts.md` states it directly: \"All legacy workflow functionality has been replaced by Workflow Studio content… While all customers retain the ability to create legacy workflows, you are strongly encouraged to instead use Workflow Studio… Only Workflow Studio will receive new features and enhancements.\" C overstates — existing legacy workflows still run, and customers who upgraded retain the ability to edit them."},
{id:311,d:3,q:"A create ACL on a custom table has the condition `Priority is 1`. A user with the required role fills in Priority = 1 on the new-record form and submits, but is denied. The same user can create records when the condition is removed. What is happening?",opts:["Create ACLs are evaluated after the insert commits, so the condition sees the old value", "Fields on a new record are treated as empty until the record is saved, so the condition evaluates false", "Create ACLs ignore conditions and evaluate only roles", "The Priority field needs a data policy to populate it before the ACL runs"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Straight from the operations table in `platform-security/access-control/exploring-access-control-list.md`: \"A create ACL with a condition requiring that a field contain a specific value may evaluate as false. Fields on new records are considered empty until the record is saved.\" This is why create ACLs should gate on roles, and field-value checks belong in a Business Rule where the values are actually bound. Trap on A: the ACL runs *before* the insert, not after."},
{id:312,d:3,q:"A Deny-Unless ACL on a table passes for a given user. No Allow-If ACL matches that table at all. What happens?",opts:["Access is denied, because an Allow-If ACL must explicitly permit access", "Access is granted by default", "The request is denied and written to the access log for review", "The system falls back to the `*` table rule and re-evaluates"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`acl-denial-behavior.md` is explicit and counter-intuitive: \"Even if a Deny-Unless ACL matches, access is only granted when an Allow-If ACL explicitly permits it. If no Allow-If ACL is matched and the Deny-Unless ACL passes, the system grants access by default.\" So a passing Deny-Unless with nothing to contradict it lets the request through. Read the first sentence alone and you would pick A — the second sentence is the answer."},
{id:313,d:3,q:"A field-level ACL grants the `itil` role read on `mytable.description`. A user with `itil` still cannot see the field, and cannot see any other field on the table either. What is the most likely cause?",opts:["The field ACL needs to be marked active before it takes effect", "The user is failing the table-level ACL, which denies every field regardless of field rules", "Field ACLs require a matching data policy", "The `description` field is protected by a script protection policy"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`acl-rule-types.md`: \"If a user fails a table ACL rule, the user is denied access to all fields in the table, even if the user passes a field ACL rule.\" A user must pass both the table rule and the field rule. Debugging order therefore runs table-first: a field-level grant is worthless while the table rule is failing."},
{id:314,d:3,q:"An administrator creates an `add_to_list` ACL to stop users selecting a sensitive column when building reports. Users can still pick the column. What has been missed?",opts:["The `glide.report.add_to_list_supported` property has not been enabled", "The ACL needs a script as well as a role", "`add_to_list` only applies to database views", "Existing reports must be deleted before the ACL takes effect"],ans:[0],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`now-intelligence/reporting/column-view-access-control-list-reports.md`: \"the glide.report.add_to_list_supported system property enables the add_to_list access control list.\" The property gates the whole mechanism and is off by default, so the ACL sits inert until it is switched on. Two further limits from the same page: it applies only to the report's top-level table, and existing reports are unaffected — which is why D is wrong as a fix."},
{id:315,d:3,q:"A vendor publishes an application containing a Script Include with the Protected script protection policy. What can a developer on the subscriber instance do with it?",opts:["View the script logic but not change it", "Neither view nor change the script logic", "Change the script, but changes are reverted on upgrade", "View and change the script if they hold the `security_admin` role"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`application-development/c_ScriptProtectionPolicy.md` sets out three policies: None (\"allow other application developers to customize your script include\"), Read-only (\"see your script logic, but not change it\"), and Protected (\"prevent other application developers from changing your intellectual property\"). The page frames the policy as governing \"whether someone can view or edit\", so Protected withholds both — A is the Read-only setting, which is the intended confusion. ⚠️ Some sources describe Protected as *encrypting* the source; the documentation does not use that word, so do not rely on the mechanism, only the outcome."},
{id:316,d:2,q:"A form takes several seconds to render. An onLoad client script queries a reference table with a client-side GlideRecord call and populates a field from the result. What is the correct diagnosis and fix?",opts:["Client-side GlideRecord does not exist; the script cannot be doing this", "The query is valid but blocks rendering; move the lookup server-side and call it with GlideAjax", "Replace the GlideRecord call with `gs.getReference()`", "Convert the client script to a UI policy, which cannot query at all"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"A is the trap, and it is the one this bank itself got wrong (see Q97/Q294). `api-reference/api-client.md` lists \"GlideRecord - Client\", which \"enables the use of some GlideRecord functionality in client-side scripts, such as client scripts and UI policy scripts\" — so the script is legal. The problem is cost, not legality: a synchronous client query holds up rendering. The documented route to the server from a client script is GlideAjax with a client-callable Script Include (Q87)."},
{id:317,d:2,q:"A UI policy makes a field mandatory when `Category` is `Hardware`. An administrator removes `Category` from the form layout to tidy the form, expecting the policy to stop firing. It still fires. Why?",opts:["The policy is cached and will stop after the cache clears", "A UI policy condition evaluates all fields, whether or not they appear on the form", "Removing a field from the layout also removes it from the table, so the condition errors open", "UI policies re-read the layout only when the Run scripts option is on"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`platform-administration/t_CreateAUIPolicy.md`: \"A UI policy condition evaluates all fields even if they are not visible on the form. This function removes the requirement that a field must be on a form for it to be evaluated.\" Removing a field from the layout changes what is *displayed*, never what is *evaluated* — and never what exists on the table, which is why C is wrong twice over."},
{id:318,d:2,q:"A UI policy makes a field mandatory on the form. Records created through an import set arrive with that field empty. What should the developer do?",opts:["Add an onSubmit client script to the same table", "Convert the UI policy to a data policy, so the rule also applies to import sets and web services", "Add the field to the import set table's transform map as mandatory", "Nothing — UI policies already apply to imports"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`t_ConvertAUIPolicyToADataPolicy.md`: \"You can also apply a UI policy to import sets or to data imported by SOAP web services when you convert it to a data policy.\" A UI policy guards the form; a data policy guards the data whichever route it arrives by. Remember the two consequences: converting deactivates the UI policy, so tick Use as UI Policy on client to keep it working on the form, and conversion requires Run scripts off, Global on, and Visible set to Leave Alone."},
{id:319,d:4,q:"A privately scoped legacy workflow reaches an Approval activity and simply stops. No error appears, the context stays open, and the approval is never generated. What is the most likely cause?",opts:["The approval activity requires the `approver_user` role on the workflow", "A privately scoped workflow calling a common global resource such as approvals fails, either with an exception or a hung activity", "The workflow was published while the context was running, orphaning it", "Approval activities are unsupported in legacy workflow and must use Flow Designer"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`build-workflows/legacy-workflow/c_WorkflowScope.md`: \"any privately scoped workflows that make calls out to other scoped resources fail with either an exception or a hung activity while waiting for returned results\", listing ECC queues, Tasks, Approvals, Events and SLA timers. The hung-activity variant is the nastier symptom precisely because nothing errors — the context just stops advancing, which is what this scenario describes."},
{id:320,d:4,q:"A developer publishes a corrected version of a workflow. Records whose workflow contexts were already running continue to behave the old way. Is this a defect?",opts:["Yes — publishing should update all contexts, so the publish did not complete", "No — running contexts continue on the version that was current when they started; the next run uses the new version", "Yes — the old version should have been deactivated before publishing", "No — but only because the workflow was checked out at the time"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"Documented behaviour, not a defect. `c_WorkflowVersions.md`: \"the changes are not applied to running workflow contexts. Any currently running workflow context continues using the workflow version that was available when the workflow started. The next time the workflow runs, it uses the updated, published version.\" This is the version-vs-context distinction with real consequences — a fix does not reach work already in flight."},
{id:321,d:4,q:"After adding an `after` Business Rule that ends with `current.update()`, users report duplicate notification emails and the instance logs show the rule running repeatedly on the same record. What is the cause?",opts:["The notification's Weight field is set to zero", "`current.update()` in a Business Rule triggers an additional database operation, which can cause duplicate notifications and recursive loops", "An `after` rule cannot modify the current record, so the platform retries it", "The rule is missing a condition, so it runs on query as well"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`api-reference/business-rules-classic/c_BusinessRules.md` states both the rule and the symptom: \"current.update() should not be used in any Business Rules. Using current.update() triggers an additional database operation, which could cause duplicate notifications, recursive loops, etc.\" In a *before* rule you simply set the field and the platform saves it once; in an *after* rule, prefer a targeted GlideRecord update on the related record rather than re-saving `current`."},
{id:322,d:1,q:"A developer creates several application files over an afternoon and later finds some belong to the wrong application. What is the most likely explanation, and what visual cue would have warned them?",opts:["The files were created in the wrong update set; the update set name in the status bar would have shown it", "The application picker was set to a different scope; a non-Global scope shows the picker icon with a red ring", "The files were created before the application record was saved; no cue is available", "Delegated development moved the files automatically; the Manage Developers list would have shown it"],ans:[1],multi:false,src:"v2",exp:"`c_ApplicationContext.md`: \"When application developers create new records, the system automatically assigns the records to the currently selected application in the application picker.\" And `c_ApplicationPicker.md` gives the cue: \"When your application scope is set to a non-Global scope, the picker icon displays with a red ring.\" Trap on A: the update set is also shown in the Studio status bar and is also easy to get wrong — but an update set controls what is *captured for migration*, not which application a file *belongs to*. Two different mistakes with two different symptoms."}
];
